WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:03.236
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:11.244
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:56.222
[MUSIC - "MAYBE THIS TIME"]

00:00:57.456 --> 00:01:01.995
(SINGING) Maybe this
time I'll be lucky.

00:01:02.161 --> 00:01:04.798
Maybe this time he'll stay.

00:01:06.465 --> 00:01:17.911
Maybe this time, for the first
time, love won't hurry away.

00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:23.750
He will hold me fast.

00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:31.291
I'll be home at last.

00:01:33.592 --> 00:01:44.070
Not a loser anymore like the
last time and the time before.

00:01:45.771 --> 00:01:52.178
Everybody loves a winner.

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:57.450
So nobody loved me.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:01.887
(TALKING) I call
singing my first language.

00:02:02.588 --> 00:02:07.627
Our currency is being open
and being open and available

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:10.329
to our emotions.

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:12.198
If you're closed
off as a person,

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:14.333
how are you going to be a
performer to get out there

00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:18.471
and, you know, show love or
show agony or show despair?

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:21.207
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:26.546
Listen, make sure I'm
listening to the person I'm

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:29.548
up there on stage with, because
if you're truly listening,

00:02:29.715 --> 00:02:32.418
then that'll force you
to be in the moment.

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:35.388
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:38.324
[MUSIC - "GOD BLESS THE CHILD"]

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:46.032
(SINGING) Yes, the strong gets
more while the weak ones fade.

00:02:47.366 --> 00:02:54.240
Empty pockets don't
ever make the grade.

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:59.178
Mama may have.

00:02:59.178 --> 00:03:02.715
Papa may have.

00:03:02.915 --> 00:03:09.689
But God bless the child
that's got his own.

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:13.059
 That's got his own.

00:03:14.293 --> 00:03:18.931
(TALKING) Evolution is very
important to me as an artist.

00:03:19.565 --> 00:03:23.335
Usually, if I'm scared of
it, I know I have to do it.

00:03:23.769 --> 00:03:28.107
I will be a better
actress, a better artist.

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:32.345
It doesn't necessarily
mean that I will succeed.

00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:35.247
But will I have
grown as a result?

00:03:35.247 --> 00:03:36.749
[MUSIC - "BESS YOU IS MY WOMAN"]

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:40.919
(SINGING) From this minute, I'm
telling you, I keep this vow.

00:03:40.919 --> 00:03:45.925
Oh my Bessie, we is happy now.

00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:56.302
We is one now.

00:03:57.469 --> 00:04:02.242
APPLAUSE]

00:04:38.844 --> 00:04:42.448
Good afternoon.

00:04:42.515 --> 00:04:43.949
I'm Philip Khoury.

00:04:43.982 --> 00:04:46.853
And it is my great pleasure
to welcome all of you

00:04:46.853 --> 00:04:54.426
to the 2018 Eugene McDermott
Award in the Arts Celebration--

00:04:54.827 --> 00:05:01.400
celebration of the
extraordinary Audra McDonald.

00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:05.271
And if any of you were at
Symphony Hall last night

00:05:05.271 --> 00:05:09.308
and had the privilege
of seeing and listening

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:15.481
to Audra McDonald, you'll know
just how extraordinary she is.

00:05:16.281 --> 00:05:21.020
First, I want to recognize Mary
McDermott-Cook who is with us

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:23.189
this evening.

00:05:23.189 --> 00:05:26.225
Mary McDermott-Cook is the
president of the Eugene

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:28.160
McDermott Foundation.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:31.997
She has been an
inspiration to all of us

00:05:31.997 --> 00:05:36.369
at MIT for her great
support and in support

00:05:36.369 --> 00:05:41.374
of her remarkable mother,
Margaret McDermott.

00:05:42.574 --> 00:05:45.411
Margaret McDermott
is the individual

00:05:45.411 --> 00:05:50.416
who created this
award 44 years ago.

00:05:52.051 --> 00:05:55.755
And it has been a
thriving award throughout.

00:05:55.755 --> 00:05:57.723
And Margaret
couldn't be with us,

00:05:57.723 --> 00:06:01.728
but Mary is always with us,
here representing her mother

00:06:01.728 --> 00:06:06.265
and also here with members
of friends and members

00:06:06.265 --> 00:06:07.766
of the McDermott family.

00:06:07.766 --> 00:06:11.003
So we're just delighted, Mary,
for your tremendous support,

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:13.072
and we wish Margaret
were here with us.

00:06:13.072 --> 00:06:17.843
And I know you'll wish her,
all of us, the best for her.

00:06:17.843 --> 00:06:22.281
Now as associate provost
with responsibility

00:06:22.281 --> 00:06:27.286
for the arts at MIT,
I'm reminded every day

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:32.492
of the importance the arts are
to the pursuit of excellence

00:06:33.759 --> 00:06:36.328
at this great
institution of ours.

00:06:36.328 --> 00:06:38.698
Our unparalleled
public art collection

00:06:38.698 --> 00:06:41.467
is one example but also
the concerts, the plays,

00:06:41.467 --> 00:06:44.269
the exhibitions,
the installations

00:06:44.269 --> 00:06:49.275
put on by our performing and
visual artists on our faculty

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:53.212
and among our students,
not just for this campus,

00:06:53.212 --> 00:06:55.114
but for the world.

00:06:55.114 --> 00:06:57.650
Our students who
mainly come here

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:00.919
to study science
and engineering also

00:07:00.919 --> 00:07:05.090
partake and participate
in the arts fully.

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:08.794
Nearly half of all
undergraduates at MIT,

00:07:08.794 --> 00:07:12.865
for example, choose to take a
class in the performing arts

00:07:12.865 --> 00:07:15.000
each academic year.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:18.504
That is one reason it is
felt so appropriate to have

00:07:18.504 --> 00:07:22.241
had Audra McDonald in
our midst and especially

00:07:22.241 --> 00:07:27.079
for our vocal students to have
had the opportunity to perform

00:07:27.079 --> 00:07:29.015
and be coached by her.

00:07:29.015 --> 00:07:32.918
And she paid tribute
to them last night

00:07:32.918 --> 00:07:37.223
in remarkable ways,
which we'll never forget.

00:07:37.223 --> 00:07:41.359
Now Audra McDonald is one of the
most accomplished actor-singers

00:07:41.359 --> 00:07:43.629
of the American musical theater.

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:46.899
Of course, MIT is
not the only one who

00:07:46.899 --> 00:07:51.203
has noticed her over the years.

00:07:51.203 --> 00:07:54.106
We're not the last, but
we aren't the first.

00:07:54.106 --> 00:07:59.111
You could call Audra a
serial award winner--

00:08:00.546 --> 00:08:03.616
six Tonys, two Grammys,
an Emmy, a National Medal

00:08:03.616 --> 00:08:07.920
for the Arts from President
Obama, and much more.

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:11.724
I should make clear that she has
earned the McDermott Award not

00:08:11.724 --> 00:08:16.194
for a single work or for
lifetime achievement,

00:08:16.194 --> 00:08:18.430
but for certain
qualities that reflect

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:21.334
MIT'S values and ideals.

00:08:21.334 --> 00:08:24.770
First, her commitment
to risk taking,

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:29.775
for embracing and mastering
the most challenging roles,

00:08:31.176 --> 00:08:34.847
especially, as she said,
those roles that scare her.

00:08:36.048 --> 00:08:37.916
As one critic wrote,
she immerses herself

00:08:37.916 --> 00:08:41.286
in roles with such
unrelenting fortitude

00:08:41.286 --> 00:08:44.490
that it simply knocks you out.

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:49.428
A second quality is Audra
McDonald's versatility,

00:08:49.428 --> 00:08:53.032
which reveals equally
well in performing

00:08:53.032 --> 00:08:56.435
musical theater, in The
Great American Songbook,

00:08:56.435 --> 00:09:00.873
in Shakespeare, and most
recently in network television.

00:09:00.873 --> 00:09:03.475
And a third quality
is her ability

00:09:03.475 --> 00:09:06.979
to connect creative minds
across disciplines-- something

00:09:06.979 --> 00:09:10.782
that we are totally
committed to at MIT.

00:09:10.782 --> 00:09:13.352
She commented, upon
accepting our invitation

00:09:13.352 --> 00:09:16.856
to receive the McDermott
Award, "My greatest hope

00:09:16.856 --> 00:09:20.192
is that art helps
us, as a society,

00:09:20.192 --> 00:09:22.194
to find common ground--

00:09:22.194 --> 00:09:26.732
to create, dialogue, and
to understand each other

00:09:26.732 --> 00:09:30.136
in new and meaningful ways."

00:09:30.136 --> 00:09:33.973
Now we're thrilled that
Audra McDonald's colleague

00:09:33.973 --> 00:09:38.110
and friend, Oskar Eustis,
has joined her today

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:39.645
to speak with us.

00:09:39.645 --> 00:09:42.815
He has been artistic director of
the Public Theater of New York

00:09:42.815 --> 00:09:47.820
since 2005 and is a professor
of dramatic arts, writing,

00:09:49.021 --> 00:09:51.456
and public policy at
New York University.

00:09:51.456 --> 00:09:53.425
The Public Theater
arguably has done

00:09:53.425 --> 00:09:57.696
more to nurture America's
best writers and directors

00:09:57.696 --> 00:10:00.799
and actors than any
other institution

00:10:00.799 --> 00:10:03.735
as well as to
democratize theater

00:10:03.735 --> 00:10:06.271
by expanding the roles,
participants, and art

00:10:06.271 --> 00:10:09.308
forms associated with it.

00:10:09.308 --> 00:10:12.411
These days, the transformation
can be summed up

00:10:12.411 --> 00:10:15.314
by mentioning one show--

00:10:15.314 --> 00:10:20.319
one word-- Hamilton, which
premiered there at the Public

00:10:21.286 --> 00:10:23.922
Theater in early 2015.

00:10:23.922 --> 00:10:26.926
But the innovation and
expansion of the performing arts

00:10:26.926 --> 00:10:29.528
that have characterized
Oskar Eustis's leadership

00:10:29.528 --> 00:10:33.365
of the Public Theater have
been in progress for years

00:10:33.365 --> 00:10:36.468
now and, no doubt, will
continue to transform theater

00:10:36.468 --> 00:10:39.171
in the United States and beyond.

00:10:39.171 --> 00:10:42.641
Audra McDonald appeared in
the Public Theater's 2004

00:10:42.641 --> 00:10:47.613
production of Henry IV and
in Twelfth Night in 2009.

00:10:48.781 --> 00:10:50.649
And I'm certain
Audra and Oskar have

00:10:50.649 --> 00:10:53.585
much more than these
productions in common

00:10:53.585 --> 00:10:56.689
when it comes to their
vision for theater

00:10:56.689 --> 00:11:00.225
and for the contributions
the arts make to society.

00:11:00.225 --> 00:11:01.693
I know they do.

00:11:01.693 --> 00:11:04.062
Together, Audra McDonald
and Oskar Eustis

00:11:04.062 --> 00:11:08.567
will discuss some of the
highlights of her career,

00:11:08.567 --> 00:11:13.572
how theatrical revivals reflect
the times, and the evolution

00:11:14.707 --> 00:11:16.642
of social attitudes and
cultural experiences

00:11:16.642 --> 00:11:21.647
and how theater has evolved to
become more inclusive and more

00:11:23.181 --> 00:11:26.719
representative of
contemporary diversity.

00:11:26.719 --> 00:11:30.588
Please join me in welcoming
Audra McDonald and Oskar

00:11:30.588 --> 00:11:31.256
Eustis.

00:11:31.256 --> 00:11:34.026
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:34.026 --> 00:11:35.027
Thank you.

00:11:35.995 --> 00:11:37.462
Thank you very much.

00:11:37.462 --> 00:11:41.901
I really, really [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:41.901 --> 00:11:43.402
You are spectacular.

00:11:43.402 --> 00:11:44.369
Thank you.

00:11:44.369 --> 00:11:45.370
Thank you so much.

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:46.338
[INAUDIBLE]

00:11:46.338 --> 00:11:47.806
Thank you so much, Philip.

00:11:47.806 --> 00:11:50.742
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:50.742 --> 00:11:55.715
Audra, I think we should get the
formalities out of the way.

00:12:00.552 --> 00:12:01.820
OK.

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:04.123
We're talking today to
the greatest singing

00:12:04.123 --> 00:12:06.258
actress in the history
of the American theater.

00:12:06.258 --> 00:12:09.728
[CHEERING]

00:12:09.728 --> 00:12:12.732
And--sorry, but it's true--

00:12:15.501 --> 00:12:17.703
and the thing that we're
going to be exploring today

00:12:17.703 --> 00:12:20.472
is we're going to try figure out
a little about how she does it,

00:12:20.472 --> 00:12:22.274
so we can imitate it?

00:12:22.274 --> 00:12:23.642
No.

00:12:23.642 --> 00:12:25.577
Just so we can
appreciate a little more.

00:12:25.577 --> 00:12:27.512
And I'd love to start--

00:12:27.512 --> 00:12:30.949
this is a research institution--

00:12:30.949 --> 00:12:32.785
and I'd love to
start with Lady Day.

00:12:32.785 --> 00:12:33.919
OK.

00:12:33.919 --> 00:12:35.754
Because your performance
in Lady Day was,

00:12:35.754 --> 00:12:38.890
I thought, absolutely
miraculous, not only

00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:41.660
the tremendous spiritual
and emotional depth

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:46.665
you brought, but also the
transformation of your voice.

00:12:47.833 --> 00:12:52.538
It was uncanny,
deeply disturbing.

00:12:53.973 --> 00:12:58.077
How do you understand
that about Billie Holiday?

00:12:58.077 --> 00:13:02.381
Well, you know, I was
always a terrible student.

00:13:02.381 --> 00:13:06.485
I was never good at
doing my homework on time

00:13:06.485 --> 00:13:07.953
and doing it thoroughly.

00:13:07.953 --> 00:13:09.488
But I was always a
good student when

00:13:09.488 --> 00:13:13.325
it came to researching roles
and my work in the theater.

00:13:13.325 --> 00:13:18.330
And with Billie Holiday, I
researched her into oblivion.

00:13:19.698 --> 00:13:24.069
I mean, I not only read
every book I could about her,

00:13:25.537 --> 00:13:30.209
I dug up every audio interview
I could find of her speaking.

00:13:31.376 --> 00:13:32.311
I mean, she there
was an interview

00:13:32.311 --> 00:13:33.545
she did with Mike Wallace.

00:13:33.545 --> 00:13:35.981
There were certain
other radio programs

00:13:35.981 --> 00:13:40.986
in Amsterdam and in Sweden
as well as in the States.

00:13:42.421 --> 00:13:45.490
There's a lot of audio
recordings from rehearsals

00:13:45.490 --> 00:13:50.029
she had with her band when
she was just at Jimmy Rowles's

00:13:50.029 --> 00:13:51.297
apartment, her--

00:13:51.297 --> 00:13:52.764
Where do you find
those recordings?

00:13:52.764 --> 00:13:57.402
All over the place-- on the
internet, the Dark Web--

00:13:57.402 --> 00:13:58.303
No, not the Dark Web.

00:13:58.303 --> 00:14:02.541
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:02.541 --> 00:14:04.576
A lot of places
in a lot of books

00:14:04.576 --> 00:14:06.311
that I read would make mention--

00:14:06.311 --> 00:14:07.245
Yeah, would give links.

00:14:07.245 --> 00:14:09.414
- -of where to find them.

00:14:09.414 --> 00:14:13.752
And it was very interesting with
those particular recordings,

00:14:13.752 --> 00:14:16.822
especially the ones where she
was rehearsing with her band,

00:14:16.822 --> 00:14:19.557
is you'd hear the interviews of
her with Mike Wallace and then

00:14:19.557 --> 00:14:22.894
her very off-guard with her
band and hear the difference,

00:14:22.894 --> 00:14:24.729
not only in lucidity--

00:14:24.729 --> 00:14:28.867
depending on how drunk
she was or wherever

00:14:28.867 --> 00:14:31.136
her mental state was--

00:14:31.136 --> 00:14:35.174
but she was also an
incredible raconteur.

00:14:35.174 --> 00:14:37.643
She was very funny when
she would tell stories.

00:14:37.643 --> 00:14:41.547
She was very good at
imitating people as well.

00:14:41.547 --> 00:14:45.750
So I did all of that aural
research-- of course,

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:49.054
all of her recordings, her
vocal recordings, her singing--

00:14:49.054 --> 00:14:51.590
and then I interviewed
everybody I

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:53.625
could find that was still
alive that knew her,

00:14:53.625 --> 00:14:54.726
that spent time with her.

00:14:54.726 --> 00:14:55.961
Wow.

00:14:55.961 --> 00:14:56.895
I talked to Dr.
Maya Angelou, who

00:14:56.895 --> 00:14:58.163
had spent some time with her.

00:14:58.163 --> 00:15:01.165
I spoke to some of
her old pianists.

00:15:01.165 --> 00:15:03.501
I spent time with her godson.

00:15:03.501 --> 00:15:05.370
And you just asked them
anecdotes or stories.

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:06.438
Everything.

00:15:06.438 --> 00:15:07.840
Everything.

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:10.175
And as the show was running,
more and more people

00:15:10.175 --> 00:15:12.510
would come to see the show
that had spent time with her.

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:15.481
So people would come
backstage and bring me

00:15:15.481 --> 00:15:18.083
little bits and pieces of
her through stories they had.

00:15:18.083 --> 00:15:22.721
One woman whose father ran and
a dive nightclub somewhere,

00:15:22.721 --> 00:15:25.490
and she was charged with going
to pick up Billie Holiday

00:15:25.490 --> 00:15:28.394
and bring her to the
club to do the show,

00:15:28.394 --> 00:15:31.863
and she told me the story of
how we had to stop and get

00:15:31.863 --> 00:15:33.265
a big, old bottle of--

00:15:33.265 --> 00:15:34.766
I think it was-- gin.

00:15:34.766 --> 00:15:36.802
And she had drank half of it by
the time they got to the club.

00:15:36.802 --> 00:15:38.470
And it was only like
a 20-minute drive.

00:15:38.470 --> 00:15:39.738
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:39.738 --> 00:15:43.308
So people would bring me
bits and pieces of her.

00:15:43.308 --> 00:15:45.510
And then finding
her actual voice,

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:48.480
I realized, the more I listened
to the audio recordings

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:50.982
of her speaking,
the more I realized

00:15:50.982 --> 00:15:53.785
that she sounded and
had a cadence quite

00:15:53.785 --> 00:15:57.255
a bit like my grandmother--
my paternal grandmother.

00:15:57.255 --> 00:16:00.659
And as a child, I used to--

00:16:00.659 --> 00:16:03.662
I mean, I did it even as an
adult until she passed away.

00:16:03.662 --> 00:16:04.596
My mom's here.

00:16:04.596 --> 00:16:06.565
My mom will remember this.

00:16:06.565 --> 00:16:10.435
I used to imitate my
grandmother and all

00:16:10.435 --> 00:16:13.571
of her daughters, my
aunts, to their faces,

00:16:13.571 --> 00:16:16.641
because I just thought it
was funny to imitate them.

00:16:16.641 --> 00:16:18.543
And sometimes they
thought it was funny,

00:16:18.543 --> 00:16:21.580
and sometimes they would
tell me to be quiet.

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:24.650
But I used to imitate
Nana all the time.

00:16:24.650 --> 00:16:27.886
And I realized one day, in
listening to Billie Holiday,

00:16:27.886 --> 00:16:31.590
I was like, she
speaks like Nana.

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:34.726
Oh, my goodness, Billie
Holiday sounds like Nana.

00:16:34.726 --> 00:16:38.930
So I started imitating Nana,
and that's how I found Billie.

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:40.398
Oh, my God.

00:16:40.398 --> 00:16:41.600
That's how I found the
vocal quality for Billie.

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:43.568
So in some ways, I
thank my grandmother,

00:16:43.568 --> 00:16:45.103
because she was my conduit--

00:16:45.103 --> 00:16:46.037
That's extraordinary.

00:16:46.037 --> 00:16:48.840
- -get through to Billie.

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:52.010
But I have to ask, in
addition to your grandmother

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:55.747
and the research, so much
about Billie Holiday was

00:16:55.747 --> 00:17:00.753
affected by her drug
dependency and substance abuse.

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:03.655
And when I watched
you on stage, it

00:17:03.655 --> 00:17:07.225
felt like I was watching
somebody who understood that

00:17:07.225 --> 00:17:09.661
really much more deeply than
your career could have allowed

00:17:09.661 --> 00:17:11.029
you to understand that.

00:17:11.029 --> 00:17:12.431
So--

00:17:12.431 --> 00:17:14.099
Well, I mean, I
did the research--

00:17:14.099 --> 00:17:14.766
not--

00:17:14.766 --> 00:17:15.701
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:15.701 --> 00:17:18.436
Hold on, hold on, hold on.

00:17:18.436 --> 00:17:19.370
News flash--

00:17:19.370 --> 00:17:20.304
I have never done heroin.

00:17:20.304 --> 00:17:21.639
We've got the headline tomorrow.

00:17:21.639 --> 00:17:22.307
No, no, no.

00:17:22.307 --> 00:17:23.241
No, no, no, no.

00:17:23.241 --> 00:17:25.210
I did not actually do heroin.

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:29.514
However, I did the research.

00:17:29.514 --> 00:17:33.385
We talked to heroin
addict survivors.

00:17:33.385 --> 00:17:35.087
We talked to--

00:17:35.087 --> 00:17:37.489
I say "we," I mean me and
Lonny Price, my director,

00:17:37.489 --> 00:17:40.224
who sort of brought the
project to me and held my hand

00:17:40.224 --> 00:17:42.261
the whole way through--

00:17:42.261 --> 00:17:47.198
we looked at lots of
footage of people,

00:17:47.198 --> 00:17:51.503
not only under the
influence, but people

00:17:51.503 --> 00:17:56.174
who had been through it and
lived to tell the story.

00:17:56.174 --> 00:17:59.311
We talked to some
methadone addicts.

00:17:59.311 --> 00:18:00.244
Wow.

00:18:00.244 --> 00:18:02.047
And so we did all that research.

00:18:02.047 --> 00:18:06.985
I also drank really heavily one
night and tried to do as much

00:18:07.752 --> 00:18:08.754
of the play as I could--

00:18:08.754 --> 00:18:11.489
not on stage, but just at home.

00:18:11.489 --> 00:18:13.191
I didn't get very far.

00:18:13.191 --> 00:18:16.395
But I got the
feeling of what that

00:18:16.395 --> 00:18:21.400
was like to work through and try
and function through that fog.

00:18:22.934 --> 00:18:27.572
And I talked to doctors too
just to get an idea of what

00:18:27.572 --> 00:18:28.740
the body is going through--

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:30.241
What would have
happened to her body.

00:18:30.241 --> 00:18:31.577
- -what happens to the body.

00:18:31.577 --> 00:18:33.378
And also, it's a
very specific time

00:18:33.378 --> 00:18:35.447
when the play takes place--

00:18:35.447 --> 00:18:37.482
four months before Billie
Holiday passed away.

00:18:37.482 --> 00:18:42.454
So she was dealing with very
specific medical issues.

00:18:42.454 --> 00:18:44.690
She was quite swollen.

00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:47.325
She was not eating
really at all.

00:18:47.325 --> 00:18:49.927
Her legs and her ankles
were completely swollen.

00:18:49.927 --> 00:18:52.297
Her stomach was very
distended-- all of that.

00:18:52.297 --> 00:18:54.633
So I had all of
that as reference.

00:18:54.633 --> 00:18:57.169
So it's like putting on big, hes

00:19:01.973 --> 00:19:06.244
and then trying to function
and walk through life.

00:19:06.244 --> 00:19:08.980
So that helped me
with the physicality.

00:19:08.980 --> 00:19:12.417
And all of that, I
feel like, makes sense

00:19:12.417 --> 00:19:14.986
in terms of what I saw
in your performance.

00:19:14.986 --> 00:19:19.992
What I also saw was an emotional
rawness that was breathtaking.

00:19:22.461 --> 00:19:25.063
It made me feel
so self-conscious almost to be

00:19:28.366 --> 00:19:30.402
watching somebody that--

00:19:30.402 --> 00:19:34.840
how do you do that eight times
a w-- did you do it eight times

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:36.074
a week or seven?

00:19:36.074 --> 00:19:37.509
I did it eight times
a week in London.

00:19:37.509 --> 00:19:40.044
I only did it seven
times a week on Broadway.

00:19:40.044 --> 00:19:44.415
I did it eight times a week
in London, and I had a seven-,

00:19:44.415 --> 00:19:47.586
eight-month-old baby with me.

00:19:47.586 --> 00:19:49.388
So that helped the
whole fatigue--

00:19:49.388 --> 00:19:50.188
Sand bag, sand bag.

00:19:50.188 --> 00:19:51.390
- -aspect of it.

00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:54.593
That was quite real.

00:19:54.593 --> 00:19:57.062
But, of course, the
emotional open, sometime,

00:19:57.062 --> 00:19:58.797
is characteristic of your work.

00:19:58.797 --> 00:20:01.900
It's a thing.

00:20:03.534 --> 00:20:08.473
Yeah, you know, it's
interesting when a lot of times

00:20:08.473 --> 00:20:12.677
reporters will ask me or someone
is interviewing will ask me,

00:20:12.677 --> 00:20:14.345
"What did you learn
about Billie Holiday

00:20:14.345 --> 00:20:16.181
that you didn't know that
surprised you the most?"

00:20:16.181 --> 00:20:18.116
And I think what
surprised me the most--

00:20:18.116 --> 00:20:20.752
and this is going to 
sound odd, and I'm

00:20:23.021 --> 00:20:26.324
realizing it as I'm saying it--

00:20:26.324 --> 00:20:30.162
what surprised me
the most, but maybe

00:20:30.162 --> 00:20:32.698
unlocked a key to
everything else

00:20:32.698 --> 00:20:35.066
making sense about
Billie Holiday,

00:20:35.066 --> 00:20:37.702
was how much she wanted
children and how much

00:20:37.702 --> 00:20:39.238
she wanted to be a mother.

00:20:39.238 --> 00:20:41.372
More than anything in the
world, she wanted that.

00:20:41.372 --> 00:20:42.340
She wanted family.

00:20:42.340 --> 00:20:43.674
She didn't really have it.

00:20:43.674 --> 00:20:45.243
And she wanted it
more than anything.

00:20:45.243 --> 00:20:48.579
She tried to adopt twice and
was obviously turned down.

00:20:48.579 --> 00:20:51.349
Was devastated by that.

00:20:51.349 --> 00:20:55.720
And so a lot of the sort
of loneliness and sadness

00:20:55.720 --> 00:20:58.056
that she felt-- and especially
because I am a mother.

00:20:58.056 --> 00:21:02.661
I do have that joy and
that blessing in my life--

00:21:02.661 --> 00:21:06.231
I was able to tap into
what that would be like.

00:21:06.231 --> 00:21:09.867
This woman wanted that so
desperately and didn't have it.

00:21:09.867 --> 00:21:12.737
And for some reason, that
just kind of was the key

00:21:12.737 --> 00:21:15.206
into the bottom of
her sadness for me.

00:21:15.206 --> 00:21:18.043
She shrugs
as if that's-- so what
you've described it is

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:26.317
a level of research into her,
into her medical condition,

00:21:26.317 --> 00:21:29.988
into the people knew her, into
the spiritual heart of her,

00:21:29.988 --> 00:21:32.256
and into your
grandmother's voice.

00:21:32.256 --> 00:21:34.826
And part of
what strikes me is what

00:21:36.795 --> 00:21:39.331
an enormous amount
of work that is that

00:21:39.331 --> 00:21:42.100
is not happening just
in the rehearsal hall.

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:44.236
It's happening as you're
creating the part.

00:21:44.236 --> 00:21:49.207
Yeah, yeah, I mean, that's
the life of an actor.

00:21:50.676 --> 00:21:52.643
I did an interview with
one of the MIT students--

00:21:52.643 --> 00:21:54.612
a great girl who I've
spent a bit of time

00:21:54.612 --> 00:21:57.015
with in my residency
here-- named Talia.

00:21:57.015 --> 00:22:01.486
She has a little video series
called Tea with Teachers.

00:22:01.486 --> 00:22:03.755
And so we were just recording
that, and we had some tea.

00:22:03.755 --> 00:22:05.623
And she had little
Reese's peanut butter cups

00:22:05.623 --> 00:22:09.227
out there for me too,
which made me very happy.

00:22:09.227 --> 00:22:14.232
But I was telling her that
being an artist, especially

00:22:15.634 --> 00:22:19.104
when you're being an actor,
the research is just as intense

00:22:19.104 --> 00:22:24.109
as if you were researching about
nano molecular archaeological

00:22:26.043 --> 00:22:26.745
hoo-ha.

00:22:26.745 --> 00:22:28.847
Hoo-ha, yeah.

00:22:28.847 --> 00:22:30.115
I've looked into that too.

00:22:30.115 --> 00:22:31.783
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:31.783 --> 00:22:35.119
But you have to be that
thorough with your research.

00:22:35.119 --> 00:22:36.655
I mean.

00:22:36.655 --> 00:22:38.456
If you're going to go out
there and be this person,

00:22:38.456 --> 00:22:43.328
you have to know everything that
you possibly can about them.

00:22:43.328 --> 00:22:45.629
We are not going
to focus solely on

00:22:45.629 --> 00:22:47.565
your drug-addicted characters.

00:22:47.565 --> 00:22:48.499
However--

00:22:48.499 --> 00:22:52.804
However, you rather famously--

00:22:52.804 --> 00:22:57.776
your Bess-- both the addiction
felt realer than any Bess

00:22:58.976 --> 00:23:00.812
that I had ever read
about or seen before.

00:23:00.812 --> 00:23:04.082
But you are also struggling,
not with a real person,

00:23:04.082 --> 00:23:08.519
but with a fictional character
with a very problematized

00:23:08.519 --> 00:23:10.254
history.

00:23:10.254 --> 00:23:12.790
Can you talk-- because
you're reviving

00:23:12.790 --> 00:23:14.726
what is, at one and
the same moment,

00:23:14.726 --> 00:23:18.963
a classic and yet a very
problematic perspective

00:23:18.963 --> 00:23:20.898
on African-American life.

00:23:20.898 --> 00:23:23.368
I wonder if you could talk
about what that was like.

00:23:23.368 --> 00:23:26.705
Well, in portraying Bess--

00:23:26.705 --> 00:23:29.874
Porgy and Bess was
a musical or opera,

00:23:29.874 --> 00:23:31.876
depending on how you
want to refer to it,

00:23:31.876 --> 00:23:33.644
and I know there's a
debate about that all

00:23:33.644 --> 00:23:35.746
the time about what it is--

00:23:35.746 --> 00:23:39.917
that I had loved deeply
and was quite obsessed with

00:23:39.917 --> 00:23:42.520
and knew from top to bottom.

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:46.457
I could sing every single part
in my dorm room at Juilliard,

00:23:46.457 --> 00:23:48.827
and a lot of times, I
would at night to sort of

00:23:48.827 --> 00:23:50.095
put on the record--

00:23:50.095 --> 00:23:53.865
the record, yes, it was
a record at that point--

00:23:53.865 --> 00:23:55.500
and sing through
the whole thing.

00:23:55.500 --> 00:23:57.836
So when I
was offered the part, I

00:24:01.540 --> 00:24:05.042
knew I wanted to,
not only research

00:24:05.042 --> 00:24:07.244
the opera and everything as
much as I possibly could,

00:24:07.244 --> 00:24:11.315
but I wanted to really make
sure I understood the genesis

00:24:11.315 --> 00:24:14.419
for who this woman was.

00:24:14.419 --> 00:24:16.988
And so I went back to the book.

00:24:16.988 --> 00:24:19.256
I went back to the
source material, which

00:24:19.256 --> 00:24:21.693
is the book by DuBose Heyward.

00:24:21.693 --> 00:24:24.362
And I really studied
this character.

00:24:24.362 --> 00:24:27.131
And there were certain things in
the book that obviously didn't

00:24:27.131 --> 00:24:29.867
make it into the libretto,
which were interesting to me--

00:24:29.867 --> 00:24:33.438
the fact that she had a
huge scar on her face,

00:24:33.438 --> 00:24:36.174
and that's one of the first ways
that DuBose Heyward describes

00:24:36.174 --> 00:24:37.408
her.

00:24:37.408 --> 00:24:41.680
She's this statuesque
woman, buxom woman,

00:24:43.147 --> 00:24:46.418
a little bit past her prime,
humongous scar on her face,

00:24:46.418 --> 00:24:47.919
which told a story.

00:24:47.919 --> 00:24:49.520
I mean, I think he even
refers to it in that way,

00:24:49.520 --> 00:24:51.923
that there is a story there.

00:24:51.923 --> 00:24:56.094
And so I was trying to just
get right down to the bones.

00:24:56.094 --> 00:24:58.295
And then I did the
research about what

00:24:58.295 --> 00:25:00.531
it was like to be an
African-American woman

00:25:00.531 --> 00:25:02.434
at that time and what
your choices were.

00:25:02.434 --> 00:25:05.436
Your choices-- they
were very limited.

00:25:05.436 --> 00:25:10.308
Your choices were-- you had to
have some source of income--

00:25:10.308 --> 00:25:13.945
so either you were working,
or you had a husband

00:25:13.945 --> 00:25:16.848
that was supporting you.

00:25:16.848 --> 00:25:18.683
I mean, Mom, am I--

00:25:18.683 --> 00:25:20.118
in those days.

00:25:20.118 --> 00:25:21.619
I mean, not that you
were there in those days.

00:25:21.619 --> 00:25:22.287
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:22.287 --> 00:25:23.554
I'm just saying.

00:25:23.554 --> 00:25:25.356
No, you're too young for that.

00:25:25.356 --> 00:25:29.194
But for African-American
women during the time,

00:25:29.194 --> 00:25:30.795
there were just
weren't many options.

00:25:30.795 --> 00:25:33.531
So if you didn't have either one
of those things, a lot of them

00:25:33.531 --> 00:25:37.702
had to turn toward either
prostitution or men

00:25:37.702 --> 00:25:40.972
that would support
them in whatever way

00:25:40.972 --> 00:25:44.576
or whatever contract that
they could come up with.

00:25:44.576 --> 00:25:46.611
And once again, I
studied the life.

00:25:46.611 --> 00:25:50.214
I went to Catfish Row
in South Carolina.

00:25:50.214 --> 00:25:55.220
I went to the cabin
where Gershwin

00:25:56.086 --> 00:25:59.323
wrote most of Porgy and Bess.

00:25:59.323 --> 00:26:02.027
And then obviously, there was
the political ramifications.

00:26:02.027 --> 00:26:04.596
were doing
it here in Cambridge.

00:26:06.230 --> 00:26:09.267
And the New York
Times did the story.

00:26:09.267 --> 00:26:13.871
And then Mr. Sondheim
wrote a letter and about--

00:26:13.871 --> 00:26:17.208
Not his sunniest side.

00:26:17.208 --> 00:26:20.444
And it's because everybody is
very passionate about Porgy

00:26:20.444 --> 00:26:22.747
and Bess.

00:26:22.747 --> 00:26:25.684
And I think Gershwin
absolutely approached this

00:26:25.684 --> 00:26:29.687
with love and with as much
as he could give to it

00:26:29.687 --> 00:26:31.523
and as much as he could
possibly understand.

00:26:31.523 --> 00:26:33.357
But he was still
writing about a culture

00:26:33.357 --> 00:26:35.894
that he may have studied as
much as he possibly could, but--

00:26:35.894 --> 00:26:37.127
It wasn't his.

00:26:37.127 --> 00:26:38.363
- -there was a part of
it that wasn't his,

00:26:38.363 --> 00:26:39.964
that he was not ever
going to get into.

00:26:39.964 --> 00:26:44.802
And so, for us, it was very
important to make sure that we

00:26:44.802 --> 00:26:47.038
infused it in a way that had--

00:26:47.038 --> 00:26:49.874
I don't want
to choose the wrong word--

00:26:52.577 --> 00:26:57.582
but the understanding and the
nobility and pride of that

00:26:58.916 --> 00:26:59.717
people--

00:26:59.717 --> 00:27:01.219
That's the right word.

00:27:01.219 --> 00:27:02.453
- -that perhaps there was no
possible way that Gershwin

00:27:02.453 --> 00:27:03.988
could have put into
those characters,

00:27:03.988 --> 00:27:06.157
because that's not a
culture he came from.

00:27:06.157 --> 00:27:07.425
Right, right.

00:27:07.425 --> 00:27:08.860
Even though I think he
wrote them with love.

00:27:08.860 --> 00:27:10.094
So can you talk--

00:27:10.094 --> 00:27:14.866
just famously, here
in Cambridge, Bess

00:27:15.733 --> 00:27:17.835
rejects the drugs at the end?

00:27:17.835 --> 00:27:20.137
Yes, and then by the time
you came to New York,

00:27:20.137 --> 00:27:21.372
you'd changed that decision.

00:27:21.372 --> 00:27:22.807
Yes.

00:27:22.807 --> 00:27:24.709
And I mean, can you just
talk a little bit about that?

00:27:24.709 --> 00:27:25.710
Sorry if I'm--

00:27:25.710 --> 00:27:26.978
No, no, no.

00:27:26.978 --> 00:27:29.847
I mean, I think, with
Diane Paulus, who is--

00:27:29.847 --> 00:27:31.349
Brilliant, brilliant, brilliant.

00:27:31.349 --> 00:27:33.451
- -a brilliant director
and in charge of the ART.

00:27:33.451 --> 00:27:38.189
We had been given this charge
from the estate to--

00:27:42.260 --> 00:27:45.163
all right, dust it off, see
what you can find there.

00:27:45.163 --> 00:27:48.098
And so I think we were
throwing everything at the wall

00:27:48.098 --> 00:27:51.002
and seeing what worked.

00:27:51.002 --> 00:27:52.803
So Diane wanted to
try it this way.

00:27:52.803 --> 00:27:57.308
And directors who have
worked with me have, I think,

00:27:57.308 --> 00:28:02.280
lovingly and honestly
said that I'm not--

00:28:05.082 --> 00:28:06.718
OK, I guess I'm
difficult to work with.

00:28:06.718 --> 00:28:07.652
I'm going to say it.

00:28:07.652 --> 00:28:10.188
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:10.188 --> 00:28:11.389
I will try something.

00:28:11.389 --> 00:28:12.490
I've heard "intense."

00:28:12.490 --> 00:28:13.291
Intense.

00:28:13.291 --> 00:28:14.225
I've heard demanding.

00:28:14.225 --> 00:28:17.194
I haven't heard difficult.

00:28:17.194 --> 00:28:19.063
I will try anything once.

00:28:19.063 --> 00:28:20.798
And I will try.

00:28:20.798 --> 00:28:25.804
And if you cannot convince me
of an organic and truthful why,

00:28:28.673 --> 00:28:30.208
it's never going to work on me.

00:28:30.208 --> 00:28:31.141
Right, right.

00:28:31.141 --> 00:28:33.844
I won't be able to execute it.

00:28:33.844 --> 00:28:35.146
I won't be able to do it.

00:28:35.146 --> 00:28:38.216
And as many times
as I tried to do it,

00:28:38.216 --> 00:28:42.887
rejecting the drugs in
the end, it wouldn't work.

00:28:42.887 --> 00:28:44.922
She'd watch the
show and come back

00:28:44.922 --> 00:28:48.292
and go, "OK, can
you try--" And it

00:28:48.292 --> 00:28:49.760
wasn't that I wouldn't do it.

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:53.064
It's just that I couldn't
find the why anywhere

00:28:53.064 --> 00:28:54.765
within my bones.

00:28:54.765 --> 00:28:57.635
And I guess that makes me a
bit difficult to work with.

00:28:57.635 --> 00:28:58.870
No, it doesn't.

00:28:58.870 --> 00:29:02.573
And so in the end,
Diane and I decided,

00:29:02.573 --> 00:29:03.941
OK, let's try it this way.

00:29:03.941 --> 00:29:05.242
And then we tried it that way.

00:29:05.242 --> 00:29:07.212
And it was like,
OK, there she is.

00:29:07.212 --> 00:29:08.446
There you are.

00:29:08.446 --> 00:29:12.583
And now, Audra,
it's making sense,

00:29:12.583 --> 00:29:14.986
and it's ringing true
organically for you

00:29:14.986 --> 00:29:16.520
in your performance.

00:29:16.520 --> 00:29:21.259
And it also, frankly, in New
York, it was heartbreaking.

00:29:22.360 --> 00:29:24.061
It was devastating.

00:29:24.061 --> 00:29:28.232
And that very question that you
have ennobling the character--

00:29:28.232 --> 00:29:30.367
weirdly enough, she
was more ennobled

00:29:30.367 --> 00:29:33.638
falling than she was
resisting, because you

00:29:33.638 --> 00:29:37.375
could feel the size of the
spirit that was going under.

00:29:37.375 --> 00:29:39.177
It was just, I
thought, extraordinary.

00:29:39.177 --> 00:29:41.946
It's interesting to have
played those characters back

00:29:41.946 --> 00:29:46.951
to back-- to play Bess and then
to have played Billie Holiday.

00:29:48.753 --> 00:29:53.758
James Baldwin wrote about
Billie Holiday's death right

00:29:54.625 --> 00:29:56.260
around the time of her death.

00:29:56.260 --> 00:29:58.095
Seeing her in that
cafe [INAUDIBLE]..

00:29:58.095 --> 00:30:01.132
Yes, and then he wrote about
seeing her in the hospital.

00:30:01.132 --> 00:30:06.137
And she said that she would die
if people would only leave her

00:30:06.704 --> 00:30:07.972
alone.

00:30:07.972 --> 00:30:10.542
But he also said I am
told-- this is awful--

00:30:10.542 --> 00:30:14.078
but he said, "I am told
that Dorothy Dandridge is

00:30:14.078 --> 00:30:15.346
an actress.

00:30:15.346 --> 00:30:17.281
I have yet to see
that be the case."

00:30:17.281 --> 00:30:20.451
This is what James
Baldwin said, because he

00:30:20.451 --> 00:30:23.320
felt and a lot of people felt
that Billie Holiday should have

00:30:23.320 --> 00:30:24.888
played that part,
that it should have

00:30:24.888 --> 00:30:27.425
been given to Billie Holiday,
that she knew who Bess was.

00:30:27.425 --> 00:30:28.926
She understood that.

00:30:28.926 --> 00:30:31.529
She understood everything about
what Bess was going through.

00:30:31.529 --> 00:30:32.197
That's really astonishing.

00:30:32.197 --> 00:30:33.430
It was her life.

00:30:33.430 --> 00:30:34.632
And that Bess should
have played that role.

00:30:34.632 --> 00:30:36.167
And James Baldwin
was a friend of Bess.

00:30:36.167 --> 00:30:38.535
And therefore, that's why he
was a bit scathing in saying

00:30:38.535 --> 00:30:40.471
that about Dorothy Dandridge.

00:30:40.471 --> 00:30:41.739
Wow.

00:30:41.739 --> 00:30:43.541
I mean, the image
of her playing--

00:30:43.541 --> 00:30:45.209
Yeah.

00:30:45.209 --> 00:30:48.379
But speaking of-- well, you had
a lighter role in the show--

00:30:48.379 --> 00:30:50.915
but Carousel, which
is coming back to us

00:30:50.915 --> 00:30:54.151
and which was the first
time that I and most

00:30:54.151 --> 00:30:57.922
of the world actually fully
realized how great you were

00:30:57.922 --> 00:31:01.292
is in that production, which
you were just amazing in.

00:31:01.292 --> 00:31:02.994
But Carousel is
also problematic.

00:31:02.994 --> 00:31:07.865
I mean, we had a little
celebration of wife beating.

00:31:07.865 --> 00:31:11.736
And sometimes, it
doesn't feel like--

00:31:11.736 --> 00:31:15.239
so what does it
feel like to hear

00:31:15.239 --> 00:31:16.474
it's coming back to Broadway.

00:31:16.474 --> 00:31:17.708
Yeah, to see it now--

00:31:17.708 --> 00:31:18.909
it's interesting.

00:31:18.909 --> 00:31:22.046
When we were doing
carousel back in 1994,

00:31:22.046 --> 00:31:23.981
one of the nights that
we were doing the show

00:31:23.981 --> 00:31:28.987
was the night of the infamous
slow speed white Bronco chase

00:31:30.188 --> 00:31:30.822
down the 405--

00:31:30.822 --> 00:31:31.755
Wow.

00:31:31.755 --> 00:31:34.325
- -with OJ Simpson on the run.

00:31:34.325 --> 00:31:36.894
And it was so interesting.

00:31:36.894 --> 00:31:39.363
We were all just--

00:31:39.363 --> 00:31:41.299
our hackles were all
up as we were getting

00:31:41.299 --> 00:31:43.534
ready to start the show that
night, because everybody

00:31:43.534 --> 00:31:46.170
was watching what was happening
in these places we got to go.

00:31:46.170 --> 00:31:51.141
And then to step into this
world where you've got

00:31:51.141 --> 00:31:55.446
this domestic abuse situation.

00:31:55.446 --> 00:31:58.148
And it's interesting.

00:31:58.148 --> 00:32:00.517
I think what I was saying to--

00:32:00.517 --> 00:32:02.886
Oskar and I were talking about
this a little bit backstage.

00:32:02.886 --> 00:32:06.691
With that show, just
because a character says

00:32:06.691 --> 00:32:09.793
something doesn't necessarily
mean that it's true.

00:32:09.793 --> 00:32:11.095
Right.

00:32:11.095 --> 00:32:13.564
And so I think, for some
people that are saying,

00:32:13.564 --> 00:32:15.065
"Why are they doing
Carousel again?

00:32:15.065 --> 00:32:17.534
It's about a wife
beater," and then, "She

00:32:17.534 --> 00:32:18.803
seems to think it's fine.

00:32:18.803 --> 00:32:21.372
And she says it's fine."

00:32:21.372 --> 00:32:23.173
Why are we doing that
in the age of me too,

00:32:23.173 --> 00:32:25.243
and how could you do that?"

00:32:25.243 --> 00:32:28.813
This is how certain people
in this particular situation

00:32:28.813 --> 00:32:30.281
are dealing with it.

00:32:30.281 --> 00:32:32.783
Just because Julie says, "Yes,
it's possible for somebody

00:32:32.783 --> 00:32:35.786
to hit you, hit you really
hard, and it not hurt at all."

00:32:35.786 --> 00:32:38.489
Julie can say that and
not necessarily mean it

00:32:38.489 --> 00:32:40.624
but realize she has
to say something

00:32:40.624 --> 00:32:43.627
to this child who has had
this emotional experience.

00:32:43.627 --> 00:32:46.730
And when we did Carousel,
to watch Sally Murphy,

00:32:46.730 --> 00:32:50.534
the girl who played that part,
when she said those words,

00:32:50.534 --> 00:32:53.571
or even when she's saying,
"What's the use of wondering?"

00:32:53.571 --> 00:32:57.207
It wasn't just, (SINGING)
"Yeah, he beats me,

00:32:57.207 --> 00:32:59.309
and it's really
great, and I love him.

00:32:59.309 --> 00:33:00.611
Here's is a high note!"

00:33:00.611 --> 00:33:02.346
She didn't sing it
like that at all.

00:33:02.346 --> 00:33:07.318
She sang it from a place
of anger at herself.

00:33:08.752 --> 00:33:11.489
She sang it from a place of
just being resigned that this

00:33:11.489 --> 00:33:13.624
is the life that I have chosen.

00:33:13.624 --> 00:33:16.060
And she sang it from a
place of, and in the end,

00:33:16.060 --> 00:33:18.629
throughout all of it,
I still love this man.

00:33:18.629 --> 00:33:20.697
And I can't figure out why.

00:33:20.697 --> 00:33:22.500
But I do.

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:24.101
And that's real.

00:33:24.101 --> 00:33:29.107
And that's real today for women
and certain people in 2018.

00:33:30.307 --> 00:33:31.675
And so that's why,
I think, Carousel

00:33:31.675 --> 00:33:33.210
can still have all the
relevance that it needs to have.

00:33:33.210 --> 00:33:35.346
And I don't think you
need to shy away from it.

00:33:35.346 --> 00:33:38.348
I mean, you can shine a light
on it all, but that once again,

00:33:38.348 --> 00:33:41.119
will show us-- like theater
is brilliant at doing--

00:33:41.119 --> 00:33:45.723
is how you one person's
experience of something--

00:33:45.723 --> 00:33:47.124
What you're
describing, what's so

00:33:47.124 --> 00:33:50.795
interesting is that it's
not shying away from--

00:33:50.795 --> 00:33:53.164
Not shying away from it.
- -and trying to polish up--

00:33:53.164 --> 00:33:55.232
No, no, no, no. --how ugly
the emotion is, but it

00:33:55.232 --> 00:33:56.633
is creating perspective on it.

00:33:56.633 --> 00:33:57.868
Perspective, yeah.

00:33:57.868 --> 00:33:59.703
It is saying we don't
have to simply buy

00:33:59.703 --> 00:34:01.205
this as God's point of view.

00:34:01.205 --> 00:34:02.006
No.

00:34:02.006 --> 00:34:03.741
We see it in context.

00:34:03.741 --> 00:34:07.712
Yes, and then you can really
weep for that person, saying

00:34:07.712 --> 00:34:11.682
"Oh, God," that you could
save yourself or that someone

00:34:11.682 --> 00:34:12.983
could save you from that.

00:34:12.983 --> 00:34:14.185
You know, it's interesting.

00:34:14.185 --> 00:34:15.352
That's exactly
what you're saying

00:34:15.352 --> 00:34:17.321
about Bess in
Porgy and Bess too,

00:34:17.321 --> 00:34:20.024
which is that the
way that you alter it

00:34:20.024 --> 00:34:21.726
is not by making
a happy ending--

00:34:21.726 --> 00:34:22.894
No.

00:34:22.894 --> 00:34:24.295
- -is not by changing
how dark it is,

00:34:24.295 --> 00:34:27.264
but by letting us
understand how dark it is--

00:34:27.264 --> 00:34:28.199
Exactly.

00:34:28.199 --> 00:34:29.400
- -letting us understand from--

00:34:29.400 --> 00:34:30.901
Didn't they say that's
how the Greeks got

00:34:30.901 --> 00:34:33.603
it right in terms of why we have
theater in the first place is

00:34:33.603 --> 00:34:38.609
because we needed to see people
up there killing their fathers,

00:34:40.043 --> 00:34:41.779
having sex with their mothers,
whatever, going through all

00:34:41.779 --> 00:34:46.150
these huge emotional
things, because it gave us

00:34:46.150 --> 00:34:49.086
more understanding of ourselves
and gave us the catharsis

00:34:49.086 --> 00:34:49.754
that we needed.

00:34:49.754 --> 00:34:50.921
Exactly right.

00:34:50.921 --> 00:34:53.223
And what was the
quote that you were--

00:34:53.223 --> 00:34:54.491
She's setting me up now.

00:34:54.491 --> 00:34:55.259
I'm not.

00:34:55.259 --> 00:34:56.060
I'm not.

00:34:56.060 --> 00:34:57.528
In a good way.

00:34:57.528 --> 00:34:58.863
She's being-- No, I was
just saying that Aristotle--

00:34:58.863 --> 00:35:00.364
Aristotle, yeah.

00:35:00.364 --> 00:35:03.034
- -in the poetics, he has
this famous statement that,

00:35:03.034 --> 00:35:05.236
"Tragedy shows people
as better than they are,

00:35:05.236 --> 00:35:07.672
and comedy shows people
as worse than they are."

00:35:07.672 --> 00:35:09.740
And the way I've
always interpreted

00:35:09.740 --> 00:35:12.243
that is not as, say, the
New Classical French did,

00:35:12.243 --> 00:35:14.912
that that meant that tragedy
had to be about nobility.

00:35:14.912 --> 00:35:17.081
It meant that tragedy
was about people

00:35:17.081 --> 00:35:21.052
who were more willing to
follow through on their desires

00:35:21.052 --> 00:35:24.521
and face the consequences of
their desires than we are.

00:35:24.521 --> 00:35:26.757
And comedy is about
people who are

00:35:26.757 --> 00:35:29.927
more desperate to avoid the
consequences of their desires

00:35:29.927 --> 00:35:31.195
than we are.

00:35:31.195 --> 00:35:33.497
And I think this is
exactly what you're

00:35:33.497 --> 00:35:38.503
describing for Bess and
for Julie in Carousel.

00:35:40.338 --> 00:35:42.005
So Audra, I promised
that we were

00:35:42.005 --> 00:35:44.308
going to at least try to
figure out a little bit of how

00:35:44.308 --> 00:35:45.575
you can do this.

00:35:45.575 --> 00:35:47.611
How did you start doing this?

00:35:47.611 --> 00:35:48.746
I mean, your mother's here.

00:35:48.746 --> 00:35:49.680
We could ask her.

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:51.949
But where did you come from?

00:35:51.949 --> 00:35:52.750
How did this--

00:35:52.750 --> 00:35:53.551
Where did I come from?

00:35:53.551 --> 00:35:54.818
I came from my mom.

00:35:54.818 --> 00:35:56.320
That's my mom, Anna
McDonald, there.

00:35:56.320 --> 00:35:57.822
I came from her.

00:35:57.822 --> 00:35:58.755
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:58.755 --> 00:36:00.825
Yes, yes.

00:36:00.825 --> 00:36:04.529
And actually, on behalf of
the American Theater,

00:36:09.033 --> 00:36:12.403
actually, let me take this
moment to formally thank you

00:36:12.403 --> 00:36:14.872
for one of the greatest gifts
the American Theater has ever

00:36:14.872 --> 00:36:16.941
had, and we know Audra
wouldn't exist without you.

00:36:16.941 --> 00:36:19.209
So thank you.

00:36:19.209 --> 00:36:21.478
So how did you start?

00:36:21.478 --> 00:36:24.815
Did they know you
were an actress?

00:36:24.815 --> 00:36:29.820
Every day from age two on,
our daughter was an actress.

00:36:32.690 --> 00:36:36.560
Wow, from age two on,
Audra was an actress.

00:36:36.560 --> 00:36:41.566
Very animated, very emotional
about little things,

00:36:44.768 --> 00:36:49.406
deathly afraid of the
thunder, everything like that.

00:36:49.406 --> 00:36:51.308
Everything affected me
in a very big, big way.

00:36:51.308 --> 00:36:53.443
Animated and emotional
about little things--

00:36:53.443 --> 00:36:55.312
I'm sure they didn't
seem little to Audra.

00:36:55.312 --> 00:36:56.247
Huge to me.

00:36:56.247 --> 00:36:57.147
Deathly afraid of thunder.

00:36:57.147 --> 00:36:58.081
Very.

00:36:58.081 --> 00:36:59.015
I was very afraid of thunder.

00:36:59.015 --> 00:37:01.819
So how did you find the theater?

00:37:01.819 --> 00:37:05.522
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:05.522 --> 00:37:09.994
When Audra was about
eight or nine years old,

00:37:09.994 --> 00:37:13.464
my husband and I went
to celebrate our wedding

00:37:13.464 --> 00:37:14.665
anniversary--

00:37:14.665 --> 00:37:17.668
[INAUDIBLE]

00:37:19.603 --> 00:37:21.772
When Audra was about
eight or nine years old,

00:37:21.772 --> 00:37:25.108
my husband and I went
to celebrate our wedding

00:37:25.108 --> 00:37:28.779
anniversary at a
local dinner theater.

00:37:28.779 --> 00:37:33.283
And before the
actual presentation,

00:37:33.283 --> 00:37:38.289
there was a group of young
people who did a cabaret show.

00:37:39.490 --> 00:37:41.792
And they called them
The Junior Company.

00:37:41.792 --> 00:37:45.796
And after they performed-- and
we had been dealing with one

00:37:45.796 --> 00:37:49.266
of Audra's
performances at home--

00:37:49.266 --> 00:37:52.236
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:52.236 --> 00:37:55.740
- -and at
the time, my husband
was taking some child

00:37:58.776 --> 00:37:59.777
psychology classes.

00:37:59.777 --> 00:38:01.178
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:01.178 --> 00:38:02.379
He really was.

00:38:02.379 --> 00:38:03.647
And he asked his
professor, he said,

00:38:03.647 --> 00:38:05.216
"I've got this kid
at home who just--

00:38:05.216 --> 00:38:08.252
I mean, it's like she's
a parade every day."

00:38:08.252 --> 00:38:10.987
And he said, "You need
to channel that energy."

00:38:10.987 --> 00:38:14.825
Well, we were told, you might
want to put her on Ritalin,

00:38:14.825 --> 00:38:16.193
get her into
something like that,

00:38:16.193 --> 00:38:19.029
because they called
her hyperactive.

00:38:19.029 --> 00:38:22.299
And when we were sitting and saw
all of these kids performing,

00:38:22.299 --> 00:38:25.502
we looked at each
other and said, "Audra.

00:38:25.502 --> 00:38:27.705
Let's get her up there."

00:38:27.705 --> 00:38:29.173
And that's what
we decided to do.

00:38:29.173 --> 00:38:31.475
We let her audition,
and she became

00:38:31.475 --> 00:38:33.543
a part of The Junior
Company and had an outlet

00:38:33.543 --> 00:38:34.511
for all of that energy.

00:38:34.511 --> 00:38:36.080
Oh, that's so interesting.

00:38:36.080 --> 00:38:38.949
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:38.949 --> 00:38:42.787
And did you know
right away that this was what

00:38:45.655 --> 00:38:47.457
you were going to keep doing?

00:38:47.457 --> 00:38:48.259
Yeah.

00:38:48.259 --> 00:38:49.493
So how old were you?

00:38:49.493 --> 00:38:50.728
Eight or nine, nine.

00:38:50.728 --> 00:38:51.962
Wow.

00:38:51.962 --> 00:38:52.629
Eight.

00:38:52.629 --> 00:38:53.163
Yeah, eight.

00:38:53.163 --> 00:38:54.131
Yeah.

00:38:54.131 --> 00:38:55.599
And what did it feel like?

00:38:55.599 --> 00:38:58.268
I felt calm, which is crazy.

00:38:58.268 --> 00:38:58.935
No, no, no.

00:38:58.935 --> 00:38:59.603
I get it.

00:38:59.603 --> 00:39:00.804
I get it.

00:39:00.804 --> 00:39:04.308
But it's exactly what
my mom described.

00:39:04.909 --> 00:39:06.844
I had an outlet.

00:39:06.844 --> 00:39:09.379
I mean, if I was going to
freak out about the thunder,

00:39:09.379 --> 00:39:12.983
I could freak out about the
thunder while singing on stage

00:39:12.983 --> 00:39:15.352
and get paid $10
a week to do it,

00:39:15.352 --> 00:39:17.687
which is my salary at the time.

00:39:17.687 --> 00:39:20.357
I was thrilled with
the $10 a week.

00:39:20.357 --> 00:39:23.594
But yeah, and so for
me, I had an outlet.

00:39:23.594 --> 00:39:27.398
I felt normal.

00:39:29.433 --> 00:39:30.568
I felt normal.

00:39:30.568 --> 00:39:33.270
I didn't feel crazy.

00:39:33.270 --> 00:39:36.706
For me, my memory is
that I didn't feel

00:39:36.706 --> 00:39:40.043
like I was a problem anymore.

00:39:40.043 --> 00:39:42.312
And that maybe-- and I'm
sure there were more problems

00:39:42.312 --> 00:39:46.684
to come-- but in the
sense that I had a place

00:39:46.684 --> 00:39:48.585
to go and be myself.

00:39:48.585 --> 00:39:52.757
It was like it just fit.

00:39:54.058 --> 00:39:55.493
It was like that
piece in a puzzle.

00:39:55.493 --> 00:39:57.294
There was an opening in
the middle of a puzzle,

00:39:57.294 --> 00:39:58.461
and they just plugged me in.

00:39:58.461 --> 00:39:59.796
It's like, oh, OK.

00:39:59.796 --> 00:40:01.632
OK, that's why I
have the piece that

00:40:01.632 --> 00:40:03.466
looks like this
and this piece has

00:40:03.466 --> 00:40:06.003
my heart that beats
really big like that,

00:40:06.003 --> 00:40:08.005
because it fits in here.

00:40:08.005 --> 00:40:10.775
What's funny about that is
that you're the greatest

00:40:13.843 --> 00:40:16.112
performer of our
generation, and I'm just

00:40:16.112 --> 00:40:17.448
a guy who runs a theater.

00:40:17.448 --> 00:40:19.916
And that's exactly how
I felt about the theater

00:40:19.916 --> 00:40:20.850
when I went there.

00:40:20.850 --> 00:40:23.353
I felt normal for first time.

00:40:23.353 --> 00:40:25.822
I didn't feel like I was
too loud for the first time.

00:40:25.822 --> 00:40:28.391
I didn't feel like I cried
too easily for the first time.

00:40:28.391 --> 00:40:29.627
Yes, yes.

00:40:29.627 --> 00:40:31.128
I didn't feel like
I laughed too much.

00:40:31.128 --> 00:40:31.929
Yes.

00:40:31.929 --> 00:40:33.197
All of this stuff that--

00:40:33.197 --> 00:40:34.698
and I was in
Minnesota, and boy--

00:40:34.698 --> 00:40:38.335
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:38.335 --> 00:40:39.403
Fresno, you know.

00:40:39.403 --> 00:40:39.970
Yeah.

00:40:39.970 --> 00:40:41.204
Yeah.

00:40:41.204 --> 00:40:44.174
And the crying too
much thing for a boy--

00:40:44.174 --> 00:40:45.842
not good.

00:40:45.842 --> 00:40:47.645
 [INAUDIBLE]
you found your tribe.

00:40:50.714 --> 00:40:52.149
Found your tribe.

00:40:52.149 --> 00:40:55.286
And it's the beautiful
thing about the theater

00:40:55.286 --> 00:40:56.753
is that I've never
found anything

00:40:56.753 --> 00:41:00.057
that I care about that I
can't bring into the theater--

00:41:00.057 --> 00:41:02.126
any subject I'm interested
in, any feeling I

00:41:02.126 --> 00:41:03.794
have, there's a place to--

00:41:03.794 --> 00:41:05.062
There is a space for it all.

00:41:05.062 --> 00:41:05.863
There's a place to put it.

00:41:05.863 --> 00:41:07.364
Yeah, absolutely.

00:41:07.364 --> 00:41:11.502
And so as you started growing
up and went off to that very

00:41:12.670 --> 00:41:14.171
non-competitive
place, Juilliard,

00:41:14.171 --> 00:41:17.241
that very sort of accepting,
almost hippie school--

00:41:17.241 --> 00:41:20.477
[LAUGHTER] how
did that work out?

00:41:20.477 --> 00:41:21.411
Oh, great.

00:41:21.411 --> 00:41:23.246
Great.

00:41:23.246 --> 00:41:25.215
What did it feel--
because once you there,

00:41:25.215 --> 00:41:27.284
that's like serious
professional.

00:41:27.284 --> 00:41:28.519
Yes.

00:41:28.519 --> 00:41:30.921
I mean, Juilliard,
it seems like it

00:41:30.921 --> 00:41:34.991
should have been just like
right along the trajectory.

00:41:34.991 --> 00:41:39.596
But for me, I auditioned for
a couple of different schools,

00:41:39.596 --> 00:41:43.133
and I think I got into
UCLA and UC-Irvine and NYU.

00:41:43.133 --> 00:41:46.170
And my mom took me to New York
to audition for Juilliard.

00:41:46.170 --> 00:41:51.141
And I just blew the audition,
because I didn't really

00:41:51.909 --> 00:41:53.410
know about opera.

00:41:53.410 --> 00:41:55.245
And I didn't think that
that's where I would end up.

00:41:55.245 --> 00:41:56.680
But I just thought,
let me audition

00:41:56.680 --> 00:41:58.414
for the vocal department,
because I think

00:41:58.414 --> 00:42:00.950
my singing is my strong point.

00:42:00.950 --> 00:42:04.321
But I was thinking that I'd
end up in the musical theater

00:42:04.321 --> 00:42:05.823
department.

00:42:05.823 --> 00:42:07.658
There's no musical theater
department at Juilliard.

00:42:07.658 --> 00:42:10.260
But I didn't figure
that part out.

00:42:10.260 --> 00:42:11.862
So I went ahead,
and I auditioned.

00:42:11.862 --> 00:42:16.567
And I riffed at the end of
"Deh vieni, non tardar,"

00:42:16.567 --> 00:42:20.571
which is Mozart's aria
from "Le Nozze di Figaro."

00:42:20.571 --> 00:42:25.576
You don't riff with
Mozart, but I did.

00:42:26.810 --> 00:42:28.111
I said I was a
mezzo-soprano, even though I

00:42:28.111 --> 00:42:29.379
was singing a soprano aria.

00:42:29.379 --> 00:42:30.814
I was like, yeah, I'm a mezzo.

00:42:30.814 --> 00:42:32.182
And then I sang, and they're
like, "No, you're not.

00:42:32.182 --> 00:42:33.283
What are you doing?"

00:42:33.283 --> 00:42:35.452
I just was terrible.

00:42:35.452 --> 00:42:37.888
And they accepted me,
so it was like, well,

00:42:37.888 --> 00:42:39.957
Juilliard accepted me.

00:42:39.957 --> 00:42:42.125
I better say yes, especially
before they figure out

00:42:42.125 --> 00:42:43.660
that they made a mistake.

00:42:43.660 --> 00:42:45.595
So I just went.

00:42:45.595 --> 00:42:48.065
And I just thought,
well, I'm in New York.

00:42:48.065 --> 00:42:49.966
I'm close to Broadway.

00:42:49.966 --> 00:42:52.869
My address was on Broadway.

00:42:52.869 --> 00:42:53.703
Here we go.

00:42:53.703 --> 00:42:55.205
This is going to happen.

00:42:55.205 --> 00:42:59.476
And what I thought was this
trajectory felt like, [GRUNTS],

00:43:00.944 --> 00:43:05.583
because I was in the vocal
performance, which meant you

00:43:06.950 --> 00:43:10.387
study your languages, you study
your Italian, your French,

00:43:10.387 --> 00:43:13.656
your German, your Spanish,
your English diction.

00:43:13.656 --> 00:43:16.359
You take literature
and material classes.

00:43:16.359 --> 00:43:18.194
You take ear-training classes.

00:43:18.194 --> 00:43:19.830
You take all the
classical classes

00:43:19.830 --> 00:43:23.233
you need to be a
classical musician.

00:43:23.233 --> 00:43:26.837
You have your voice
lessons, and that's it.

00:43:26.837 --> 00:43:28.005
Wow.

00:43:28.005 --> 00:43:29.940
And so all of a
sudden, I felt like I

00:43:29.940 --> 00:43:32.109
made a wrong turn somewhere.

00:43:32.109 --> 00:43:35.011
And I am never going to
get to Broadway, a, and b,

00:43:35.011 --> 00:43:37.581
I hate what I sound like.

00:43:37.581 --> 00:43:39.316
I don't want to be
an opera singer.

00:43:39.316 --> 00:43:41.684
I don't like singing like this.

00:43:41.684 --> 00:43:43.587
That's not what I want to do.

00:43:43.587 --> 00:43:45.155
What's happening?

00:43:45.155 --> 00:43:47.857
It was a very
confusing time for me.

00:43:47.857 --> 00:43:49.359
Wow, Audra.

00:43:49.359 --> 00:43:54.298
But I ended up finding out that
I did have an operatic sound.

00:43:55.832 --> 00:44:00.270
And Juilliard helped me discover
a whole section of my voice

00:44:00.270 --> 00:44:02.005
that I didn't know existed.

00:44:02.005 --> 00:44:05.742
And so, once I was able to
figure all that out and then

00:44:05.742 --> 00:44:07.544
come back around
the bend, I mean,

00:44:07.544 --> 00:44:09.479
and maybe my path was
this way, and I just

00:44:09.479 --> 00:44:10.481
thought it was this way.

00:44:10.481 --> 00:44:11.715
I don't know.

00:44:11.715 --> 00:44:13.417
Yeah, or it's just life
was happening to you--

00:44:13.417 --> 00:44:14.685
Life was happening.

00:44:14.685 --> 00:44:15.919
- -and it was letting
you figure things out.

00:44:15.919 --> 00:44:17.454
Yeah, and I was in the
exact right place, but--

00:44:17.454 --> 00:44:18.688
How strange.

00:44:18.688 --> 00:44:19.823
- -the whole time
I was there, you

00:44:19.823 --> 00:44:21.324
could have never
convinced me that I was.

00:44:21.324 --> 00:44:22.559
Yeah.

00:44:22.559 --> 00:44:24.595
I mean, partly is that
your mistake started

00:44:24.595 --> 00:44:26.897
at the audition of thinking
that your voice was

00:44:26.897 --> 00:44:28.265
your strong suit.

00:44:28.265 --> 00:44:30.400
I want to point
out that Audra is

00:44:30.400 --> 00:44:33.136
the only actor in
American history

00:44:33.136 --> 00:44:37.841
who has won every possible
Tony award for Best Supporting

00:44:37.841 --> 00:44:41.744
Actress, Best Lead Actress,
Best Actress in a Musical,

00:44:41.744 --> 00:44:43.814
and Best Supporting
Actress in a Musical.

00:44:43.814 --> 00:44:45.949
I don't think your voice
is your only strong point.

00:44:45.949 --> 00:44:49.252
I mean, the jury's
in on the one.

00:44:49.252 --> 00:44:53.157
It was right
in the middle of there

00:44:55.359 --> 00:44:57.126
that you went to
Carousel, right?

00:44:57.126 --> 00:44:58.194
Or right after Juilliard?

00:44:58.194 --> 00:44:58.996
Right after.

00:44:58.996 --> 00:45:00.897
How did that happen?

00:45:00.897 --> 00:45:02.733
I had a breakdown
while I was at Juilliard.

00:45:07.003 --> 00:45:10.874
I had a bit of a mental break.

00:45:10.874 --> 00:45:12.609
I had a suicide attempt.

00:45:12.609 --> 00:45:15.078
I ended up in a mental
hospital for a month.

00:45:15.078 --> 00:45:20.084
And I took a semester and a
little more off from school.

00:45:21.818 --> 00:45:23.386
And then I went back--

00:45:23.386 --> 00:45:24.921
all supported by the school.

00:45:24.921 --> 00:45:27.557
They were very supportive.

00:45:27.557 --> 00:45:28.992
And it's nothing that I hide.

00:45:28.992 --> 00:45:31.962
I talk about it quite
openly, especially because I

00:45:31.962 --> 00:45:34.798
know a lot of young
people listen to me

00:45:34.798 --> 00:45:36.800
and follow me and
follow my career.

00:45:36.800 --> 00:45:39.670
And I want them to know
that there is a way through.

00:45:39.670 --> 00:45:43.440
And so I went back.

00:45:43.440 --> 00:45:46.809
And while I was there,
I got the opportunity

00:45:46.809 --> 00:45:51.815
to audition for Carousel.

00:45:52.615 --> 00:45:54.017
No, no, no.

00:45:54.017 --> 00:45:55.485
I got the opportunity to
audition for The Secret Garden,

00:45:55.485 --> 00:45:56.987
which was going on tour.

00:45:56.987 --> 00:45:59.289
And I auditioned.

00:45:59.289 --> 00:46:01.024
And they accepted me.

00:46:01.024 --> 00:46:03.360
And I had to ask permission
to leave school to do it.

00:46:03.360 --> 00:46:05.462
And they said,
"Yeah, go do that.

00:46:05.462 --> 00:46:08.031
We think that's probably where
you're going to end up anyway,

00:46:08.031 --> 00:46:09.165
so we're fine with it."

00:46:09.165 --> 00:46:10.200
So they let me go.

00:46:10.200 --> 00:46:11.434
Wow, wow, wow.

00:46:11.434 --> 00:46:13.470
And they said, you can
defer and just graduate

00:46:13.470 --> 00:46:15.004
when you're done with that.

00:46:15.004 --> 00:46:18.442
So I came back, graduated, went
back out on the road with that,

00:46:18.442 --> 00:46:21.345
and then auditioned for
Carousel and got Carousel.

00:46:21.345 --> 00:46:26.216
A year and two
months after I graduated,

00:46:27.884 --> 00:46:31.888
I opened in Carousel across the
street at the Vivian Beaumont.

00:46:31.888 --> 00:46:33.891
And I could literally,
from the Vivian Beaumont,

00:46:33.891 --> 00:46:36.927
look up and see my school
and some of my classmates

00:46:36.927 --> 00:46:38.895
that had gone on to get
their master's degrees.

00:46:38.895 --> 00:46:43.767
They were much smarter
than I was and whatnot.

00:46:43.767 --> 00:46:44.801
That's pretty magical.

00:46:44.801 --> 00:46:45.635
It was very magical.

00:46:45.635 --> 00:46:46.603
That's pretty extra--

00:46:46.603 --> 00:46:48.605
Very lucky.

00:46:48.605 --> 00:46:50.440
I mean, there in
the Beaumont during Carousel,

00:46:52.843 --> 00:46:57.046
did it feel like you were now
on the path you needed to be on?

00:46:57.046 --> 00:47:00.083
Did it feel right?

00:47:00.083 --> 00:47:04.154
Yes, as much as one can
be present at 23, yes.

00:47:04.154 --> 00:47:05.622
No, no, no.

00:47:05.622 --> 00:47:07.624
Do you know in that sense
that life is still happening?

00:47:07.624 --> 00:47:08.558
It's a confusing time.

00:47:08.558 --> 00:47:10.159
And I was a spastic 23-year-old.

00:47:10.159 --> 00:47:15.165
But yes, in that sense I felt
like, yes, I'm on Broadway.

00:47:16.966 --> 00:47:21.972
But I'm singing
Rodgers and Hammerstein

00:47:23.307 --> 00:47:27.177
with a pretty decent
technique in a classical way

00:47:27.177 --> 00:47:28.945
that I wouldn't
have known how to do

00:47:28.945 --> 00:47:30.881
had I not going to Juilliard.

00:47:30.881 --> 00:47:33.684
First off, I just
want thank you for talking

00:47:36.486 --> 00:47:39.389
about psychological
difficulties.

00:47:39.389 --> 00:47:40.857
That's a big deal, Audra.

00:47:40.857 --> 00:47:43.960
It's something that an awful
lot of people don't talk about.

00:47:43.960 --> 00:47:46.796
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:46.796 --> 00:47:50.601
And again,
it's partly also the
way you talk about it.

00:47:55.305 --> 00:47:59.175
It's just so clear that the
difficulties you had you

00:47:59.175 --> 00:48:01.644
have integrated and
recovered from and built

00:48:01.644 --> 00:48:05.582
not only an extraordinary career
but an extraordinary life,

00:48:05.582 --> 00:48:07.717
because I do know
her a little bit.

00:48:07.717 --> 00:48:11.287
And I will say that Audra is
proof of one of those things

00:48:11.287 --> 00:48:15.425
that the really greatest artists
are the best people I know.

00:48:15.425 --> 00:48:18.461
There are some just below that
level who can be real jerks.

00:48:18.461 --> 00:48:20.731
[LAUGHTER]

00:48:20.731 --> 00:48:23.800
But seriously, really the
greatest artists I know

00:48:23.800 --> 00:48:25.969
are just--

00:48:25.969 --> 00:48:27.504
they are that great
partly because

00:48:27.504 --> 00:48:30.673
of the size of their heart and
the integration of the spirit.

00:48:30.673 --> 00:48:32.142
So thank you for that.

00:48:32.142 --> 00:48:34.244
And maybe we could take just
a minute to talk about--

00:48:34.244 --> 00:48:37.380
I know you're really
involved in Covenant House.

00:48:37.380 --> 00:48:41.384
I know you've become
more of an activist.

00:48:41.384 --> 00:48:44.187
I think that's due to
a couple of things.

00:48:44.187 --> 00:48:48.191
I'm very aware of how lucky I am
and the opportunities that I've

00:48:48.191 --> 00:48:53.196
been given and the great
amount of luck and blessings

00:48:54.398 --> 00:48:56.032
I've had in my life.

00:48:56.032 --> 00:49:01.038
And I've always been vocal.

00:49:02.739 --> 00:49:05.909
I guess that's a good word
to use about everything.

00:49:05.909 --> 00:49:06.842
Thunderstorms.

00:49:06.842 --> 00:49:08.778
Yeah, thunderstorms, whatnot.

00:49:08.778 --> 00:49:13.784
But I think activism really
started in earnest when I--

00:49:15.518 --> 00:49:20.524
I mean, I was certainly raised
in a very democratic household.

00:49:21.691 --> 00:49:24.127
And I know my mom's
brother, Uncle Lloyd,

00:49:24.127 --> 00:49:27.631
did the sit-ins and stuff,
right, during the '60s?

00:49:27.631 --> 00:49:30.666
Yeah, because they're
from the Washington DC

00:49:30.666 --> 00:49:32.836
and Virginia area.

00:49:32.836 --> 00:49:34.337
And so that was
sort of in my blood.

00:49:34.337 --> 00:49:37.374
And on my dad's side
of the family as well.

00:49:37.374 --> 00:49:40.743
My great uncle was an activist.

00:49:40.743 --> 00:49:43.713
He actually worked with
Bobby Kennedy for a second.

00:49:43.713 --> 00:49:47.316
Bobby Kennedy, I think, was to
meet with him the day after he

00:49:47.316 --> 00:49:50.320
was assassinated, Uncle Jesse.

00:49:50.320 --> 00:49:53.022
At any rate, all that to
say that it's in my blood.

00:49:53.022 --> 00:49:57.394
And then I met my
wonderful husband, Will,

00:49:57.394 --> 00:49:58.528
who is also here.

00:49:58.528 --> 00:50:01.097
And he's an actor.

00:50:01.097 --> 00:50:03.366
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:03.366 --> 00:50:05.635
So we blended
our families, and we're

00:50:07.804 --> 00:50:09.806
raising our kids together.

00:50:09.806 --> 00:50:14.811
And Will was working
with you on Hair.

00:50:15.679 --> 00:50:16.880
Will was starring in Hair.

00:50:16.880 --> 00:50:18.381
And Oskar was producing
that for the Public

00:50:18.381 --> 00:50:23.320
and then on Broadway right
in the midst of the marriage

00:50:24.053 --> 00:50:25.555
equality movement.

00:50:25.555 --> 00:50:28.958
And Oskar and Will and all
the rest of Will's cast

00:50:28.958 --> 00:50:31.060
got very involved with that.

00:50:31.060 --> 00:50:33.730
And it was certainly
something that I

00:50:33.730 --> 00:50:35.398
was very passionate
about, being someone

00:50:35.398 --> 00:50:38.334
who actually believes in
marriage equality and LGBTQ

00:50:38.334 --> 00:50:39.802
rights.

00:50:39.802 --> 00:50:43.472
And we got very much, not caught
up, but just very involved

00:50:43.472 --> 00:50:47.077
with that movement
and did the march

00:50:47.077 --> 00:50:49.846
and did rallies
in New York City.

00:50:49.846 --> 00:50:54.784
And I went up to Albany to
lobby members of our Congress

00:50:54.784 --> 00:50:57.320
up there.

00:50:57.320 --> 00:51:00.756
And then I was
looking for a place

00:51:00.756 --> 00:51:05.662
to donate money on
opening night of Lady Day

00:51:06.996 --> 00:51:09.999
instead of giving
opening gifts to my cast,

00:51:09.999 --> 00:51:12.368
because the cast
consisted of me the band

00:51:12.368 --> 00:51:15.972
and the dog, the chihuahua.

00:51:15.972 --> 00:51:18.274
What do I get the chihuahua
for opening night?

00:51:18.274 --> 00:51:23.280
So I decided I would do
a donation somewhere.

00:51:24.480 --> 00:51:26.016
And I thought about
the Covenant House,

00:51:26.016 --> 00:51:28.218
which a lot of Broadway
folks had done some work for.

00:51:28.218 --> 00:51:32.188
And the Covenant House a
shelter for homeless youth.

00:51:32.188 --> 00:51:36.592
And there's homes in Canada
and in the United States

00:51:36.592 --> 00:51:38.628
and in Latin America as well.

00:51:38.628 --> 00:51:42.732
And so I went down to do a
donation in between shows,

00:51:42.732 --> 00:51:44.834
because Billie Holiday
was homeless for a lot

00:51:44.834 --> 00:51:48.204
of her childhood and
had a rough childhood.

00:51:48.204 --> 00:51:49.705
So I thought this
seems appropriate.

00:51:49.705 --> 00:51:53.576
So I went in, and I had a check.

00:51:53.576 --> 00:51:55.711
And it was in between
shows on a Saturday.

00:51:55.711 --> 00:52:00.083
And there was two sort
of security guards there.

00:52:00.083 --> 00:52:02.319
And they said, "We don't have
anybody here today-- it's

00:52:02.319 --> 00:52:04.019
the weekend-- who
can take a check.

00:52:04.019 --> 00:52:05.321
We're not allowed to do that.

00:52:05.321 --> 00:52:06.956
So we'll call around and see.

00:52:06.956 --> 00:52:08.358
So just have a seat."

00:52:08.358 --> 00:52:11.027
So while I was waiting,
I saw a young man

00:52:11.027 --> 00:52:13.997
come in off the street
with a trash bag.

00:52:13.997 --> 00:52:17.800
And he-- I don't know--
looked about 15 maybe 16.

00:52:17.800 --> 00:52:21.905
And he looked so world weary
and exhausted and frightened and

00:52:21.905 --> 00:52:23.806
defensive.

00:52:23.806 --> 00:52:27.243
And these two security
guards had also

00:52:27.243 --> 00:52:28.645
been kind of flirting with me.

00:52:28.645 --> 00:52:30.147
And they had been
saying, "You know

00:52:30.147 --> 00:52:34.150
you could write a check to
me," and all this stuff.

00:52:34.150 --> 00:52:36.253
And so I was, you
know, whatever.

00:52:36.253 --> 00:52:37.987
And then they saw
this kid come in,

00:52:37.987 --> 00:52:42.925
and they just leapt into action
like they were Batman and Robin

00:52:42.925 --> 00:52:45.562
and just took this kid and
said, "Hey, you're all right.

00:52:45.562 --> 00:52:46.229
Come on, man.

00:52:46.229 --> 00:52:46.896
We got you.

00:52:46.896 --> 00:52:47.564
We got you.

00:52:47.564 --> 00:52:48.231
You're OK.

00:52:48.231 --> 00:52:48.899
You're OK.

00:52:48.899 --> 00:52:49.565
What you need?

00:52:49.565 --> 00:52:50.500
Sit down.

00:52:50.500 --> 00:52:51.434
You want something to drink?

00:52:51.434 --> 00:52:52.401
You want something to eat?

00:52:52.401 --> 00:52:53.102
We're going to take care of you.

00:52:53.102 --> 00:52:54.070
We got you."

00:52:54.070 --> 00:52:56.640
And my heart blew wide open.

00:52:56.640 --> 00:53:00.510
And so I got very involved
with the Covenant House

00:53:00.510 --> 00:53:05.081
and am now on an International
Board of Directors

00:53:05.081 --> 00:53:06.549
of the Covenant House.

00:53:06.549 --> 00:53:11.321
And in fact, my plan is to share
part of the McDermott Award

00:53:12.722 --> 00:53:14.123
with the Covenant House to
help some of those kids there.

00:53:14.123 --> 00:53:15.591
[APPLAUSE]

00:53:15.591 --> 00:53:18.561
But anyway, so that's life.

00:53:18.561 --> 00:53:22.966
Thank you.

00:53:24.100 --> 00:53:27.370
It's because, as
Michelle Obama said,

00:53:27.370 --> 00:53:29.372
"Once you walk through
that door of opportunity,

00:53:29.372 --> 00:53:30.973
you don't slam that
door behind you.

00:53:30.973 --> 00:53:35.979
You reach backward and help
the next person through."

00:53:36.813 --> 00:53:39.582
And it's just important.

00:53:39.582 --> 00:53:41.483
And I know it's a way
that Will and I have

00:53:41.483 --> 00:53:42.985
been trying to raise our kids.

00:53:42.985 --> 00:53:46.289
And now we have our own little
17-month-old little monster

00:53:46.289 --> 00:53:47.490
that we have now.

00:53:47.490 --> 00:53:51.394
And if we can just
keep her out of jail.

00:53:51.394 --> 00:53:52.762
She's a wild thing.

00:53:52.762 --> 00:53:54.864
I don't know.

00:53:54.864 --> 00:53:56.232
But we're teaching
them that they

00:53:56.232 --> 00:53:58.434
have to be good
citizens of the world.

00:53:58.434 --> 00:53:59.970
And they have to give back.

00:53:59.970 --> 00:54:02.004
And so, as we learn it,
we're trying to teach it.

00:54:02.004 --> 00:54:04.040
That's all.

00:54:04.040 --> 00:54:05.508
And I just have
to take my moment

00:54:05.508 --> 00:54:09.211
to say that also it is
such a joy to watch you

00:54:09.211 --> 00:54:10.847
and Will together.

00:54:10.847 --> 00:54:14.083
Will is a man and artist
I admire so, so much.

00:54:14.083 --> 00:54:16.252
I've been so lucky to be
able to work with him.

00:54:16.252 --> 00:54:18.220
And to watch that
you two guys are

00:54:18.220 --> 00:54:23.226
able to make a life as actors
and as marriage together has

00:54:23.226 --> 00:54:24.727
been just a beautiful thing.

00:54:24.727 --> 00:54:28.131
So that's just [INAUDIBLE].

00:54:28.131 --> 00:54:33.136
But before we take
a few questions,

00:54:34.571 --> 00:54:36.172
I just have to let you know
that I have promised Audra

00:54:36.172 --> 00:54:39.842
that I'm not going to use
this opportunity just get her

00:54:39.842 --> 00:54:43.579
to sign up for shows for me.

00:54:43.579 --> 00:54:45.548
If you would like to
use this opportunity--

00:54:45.548 --> 00:54:49.218
[LAUGHTER] she didn't
say no to that.

00:54:49.218 --> 00:54:51.720
Aside from everything
we're going to do together,

00:54:51.720 --> 00:54:55.124
Audra, what do you want to do?

00:54:55.124 --> 00:54:59.262
When you look ahead, what
are the things you'd still

00:54:59.262 --> 00:55:02.298
like to try to be scared by?

00:55:02.298 --> 00:55:04.000
Everything.

00:55:04.000 --> 00:55:05.201
Seriously.

00:55:05.201 --> 00:55:08.671
I mean, I say it all
the time, but it's true.

00:55:08.671 --> 00:55:13.677
I just want to evolve as
an artist, as a person.

00:55:14.877 --> 00:55:17.380
You got to make good use
of the time we're given.

00:55:17.380 --> 00:55:21.051
And I don't want the
rest on my laurels.

00:55:21.051 --> 00:55:23.485
I don't want to
be content with--

00:55:23.485 --> 00:55:26.022
I'm grateful for the
success that I've had,

00:55:26.022 --> 00:55:27.657
but I don't want to
go, "OK, that's it.

00:55:27.657 --> 00:55:28.458
I'm done."

00:55:28.458 --> 00:55:29.558
I want to evolve.

00:55:29.558 --> 00:55:31.027
I want to be a better artist.

00:55:31.027 --> 00:55:32.161
I want to be a better wife.

00:55:32.161 --> 00:55:34.263
I want to be a better mother.

00:55:34.263 --> 00:55:37.033
Because if you're not evolving--

00:55:37.033 --> 00:55:38.835
I want to be a better
citizen of the world.

00:55:38.835 --> 00:55:43.440
Maybe I'm
going to run for office.

00:55:44.841 --> 00:55:45.641
I don't know.

00:55:45.641 --> 00:55:48.011
I mean, I don't know.

00:55:48.011 --> 00:55:50.246
[APPLAUSE]

00:55:50.246 --> 00:55:51.147
I want to do better.

00:55:52.415 --> 00:55:53.650
I know that's
grammatically not correct.

00:55:53.650 --> 00:55:55.184
Sorry MIT.

00:55:55.184 --> 00:55:57.420
I want to evolve.

00:55:57.420 --> 00:55:59.255
OK, if you do that, you
have a dispensation.

00:55:59.255 --> 00:56:02.325
You don't have to do any shows
for me if you run for office.

00:56:02.325 --> 00:56:05.127
But other than that, you do.

00:56:05.127 --> 00:56:07.597
Anyway, I'm not going to sum
up, because what instead we're

00:56:07.597 --> 00:56:09.932
going to do is we're going
to give the audience a chance

00:56:09.932 --> 00:56:11.167
to ask a few questions.

00:56:11.167 --> 00:56:13.136
I think we have microphones.

00:56:13.136 --> 00:56:14.069
Thank you.

00:56:14.069 --> 00:56:15.505
Audra, Oskar, thanks so much.

00:56:15.505 --> 00:56:17.006
So we'll open it up.

00:56:17.006 --> 00:56:19.943
[APPLAUSE]

00:56:19.943 --> 00:56:24.948
And Audra,
there is one alternative

00:56:29.518 --> 00:56:32.087
if, in the evolution,
you might want

00:56:32.087 --> 00:56:35.091
to come join our MIT faculty.

00:56:35.091 --> 00:56:36.525
OK.

00:56:36.525 --> 00:56:37.659
That too.

00:56:37.659 --> 00:56:39.462
We can talk to our president.

00:56:39.462 --> 00:56:42.198
We'd be honored.

00:56:43.766 --> 00:56:47.303
So we have microphones.

00:56:47.303 --> 00:56:48.538
And they're down here.

00:56:48.538 --> 00:56:51.007
And try to come
up and maybe just,

00:56:51.007 --> 00:56:54.811
if you would, if you have
a question, ask just one,

00:56:54.811 --> 00:56:57.614
so we give some time to others.

00:56:57.614 --> 00:56:58.614
Don't be shy either.

00:56:58.614 --> 00:56:59.649
No one has any questions.

00:56:59.649 --> 00:57:01.183
Please, anyone here?

00:57:01.183 --> 00:57:01.851
Yes, please.

00:57:01.851 --> 00:57:02.385
Thank you.

00:57:02.385 --> 00:57:02.919
Hi.

00:57:02.919 --> 00:57:03.452
Hi.

00:57:03.452 --> 00:57:05.321
Good.

00:57:05.321 --> 00:57:07.056
OK, this is really wild.

00:57:07.056 --> 00:57:08.491
You're like Loki.

00:57:08.491 --> 00:57:10.226
You are like a huge
vocal role model for me--

00:57:10.226 --> 00:57:11.427
Oh, thank you.

00:57:11.427 --> 00:57:12.361
- -in trying to develop
my own unique voice.

00:57:12.361 --> 00:57:13.295
And I really appreciate you.

00:57:13.295 --> 00:57:15.098
Oh, gosh, I'm tearing up.

00:57:15.098 --> 00:57:18.934
But basically-- sorry,
this is kind of weird.

00:57:18.934 --> 00:57:20.603
I'm like short, but
the stairs are tall.

00:57:20.603 --> 00:57:24.907
Anyways, so like I guess
every role for an actor--

00:57:24.907 --> 00:57:26.475
I don't actually act--

00:57:26.475 --> 00:57:29.412
I feel like every role is like
another life that you live.

00:57:29.412 --> 00:57:32.081
And you can't really like do
that without having some growth

00:57:32.081 --> 00:57:33.316
and change in yourself.

00:57:33.316 --> 00:57:35.551
So my question is,
out of all the rules

00:57:35.551 --> 00:57:37.920
you've ever played like
on stage or on screen,

00:57:37.920 --> 00:57:40.456
which one has changed you the
most for better or for worse?

00:57:40.456 --> 00:57:43.593
Between you
and Talia, man, I mean,

00:57:45.928 --> 00:57:48.064
she asked the most
amazing questions.

00:57:48.064 --> 00:57:48.864
I'm telling you.

00:57:48.864 --> 00:57:51.200
Now you're getting me.

00:57:51.200 --> 00:57:55.938
What has changed me the most?

00:57:55.938 --> 00:57:58.908
OK, I'm going to answer
that in a different way.

00:57:58.908 --> 00:58:03.847
I'm going to tell you the one
that was the hardest to play.

00:58:04.713 --> 00:58:05.981
It was the hardest to find.

00:58:05.981 --> 00:58:07.549
And that was Ruth in
A Raisin in the Sun.

00:58:07.549 --> 00:58:09.084
And the reason why
it was the hardest

00:58:09.084 --> 00:58:11.587
to find, because it was
the closest to me on paper.

00:58:11.587 --> 00:58:14.457
And I realized
I was blocking, because
it was too close to me.

00:58:18.161 --> 00:58:20.230
She was a mom.

00:58:20.230 --> 00:58:22.631
She was an exhausted
wife, a mom.

00:58:22.631 --> 00:58:27.369
And she said issues, was
dealing with a pregnancy and all

00:58:27.369 --> 00:58:28.303
this stuff--

00:58:28.303 --> 00:58:29.805
things that I really understood.

00:58:29.805 --> 00:58:31.173
And I was the same age as her.

00:58:31.173 --> 00:58:34.977
So I was blocked by her.

00:58:34.977 --> 00:58:37.280
I was blocked, and
I once I realized

00:58:37.280 --> 00:58:39.916
that I was blocked because
it was too close to me,

00:58:39.916 --> 00:58:41.484
it was about learning to like--

00:58:41.484 --> 00:58:43.986
so maybe that role changed me
the most, because it taught me

00:58:43.986 --> 00:58:47.857
that I had to open up and go
even deeper and go to like,

00:58:47.857 --> 00:58:50.225
what we were talking about
before-- like the third rail--

00:58:50.225 --> 00:58:52.461
we were talking about going
to that third rail, which

00:58:52.461 --> 00:58:53.795
is a scary place to be.

00:58:53.795 --> 00:58:56.298
But it's where all the truth is.

00:58:56.298 --> 00:58:58.801
And so that's the
one that taught me,

00:58:58.801 --> 00:59:01.904
I think, to go super deep.

00:59:01.904 --> 00:59:06.843
So that changed me in a good
way as an artist, I think.

00:59:08.010 --> 00:59:10.246
A role that changed
me in a bad way--

00:59:10.246 --> 00:59:13.482
I think my English
teacher in 11th grade

00:59:13.482 --> 00:59:17.053
would say it's when I
played Ava Peron in Evita

00:59:17.053 --> 00:59:20.056
at my dinner theater
that I grew up in.

00:59:20.056 --> 00:59:25.061
Because I guess I became
a bit of a shit at school.

00:59:27.063 --> 00:59:31.100
Because I was playing Evita at
night and then going to school

00:59:31.100 --> 00:59:32.001
and being like, "What?

00:59:32.001 --> 00:59:33.068
You want what from me?

00:59:33.068 --> 00:59:36.472
I'm not doing my homework."

00:59:36.472 --> 00:59:37.873
That was the worst one.

00:59:37.873 --> 00:59:39.342
I was pretty bad.

00:59:39.342 --> 00:59:42.245
Thank you very much.

00:59:42.245 --> 00:59:43.179
Thank you.

00:59:43.179 --> 00:59:44.580
Good luck with your career.

00:59:44.580 --> 00:59:47.049
[APPLAUSE]

00:59:47.049 --> 00:59:50.520
Hi.

00:59:51.621 --> 00:59:52.288
Hi.

00:59:52.288 --> 00:59:53.255
And she's gone.

00:59:53.255 --> 00:59:56.191
Thank you both for being here.

00:59:56.191 --> 00:59:58.394
You're very, very articulate.

00:59:58.394 --> 01:00:01.697
And my husband and I, who
are New Yorkers and partners

01:00:01.697 --> 01:00:03.866
with The Public
Theater, have enjoyed

01:00:03.866 --> 01:00:07.036
seeing you at Delacorte
and in other things.

01:00:07.036 --> 01:00:11.841
But I can't resist
taking up the opening

01:00:11.841 --> 01:00:14.443
to say, when are you
going to come back

01:00:14.443 --> 01:00:17.813
to The Public Theater?

01:00:17.813 --> 01:00:21.317
We'd love to see you again
at the Delacorte or downtown.

01:00:21.317 --> 01:00:26.322
Oh, man, well, Will and I
both love the Delacorte.

01:00:28.424 --> 01:00:29.892
We'd love to come back to--

01:00:29.892 --> 01:00:32.061
I mean, anything in the public--
but the Delacorte for sure.

01:00:32.061 --> 01:00:36.165
There's something so magical
about playing the Delacorte

01:00:36.165 --> 01:00:40.236
and being out in the open and
doing Shakespeare or whatever

01:00:40.236 --> 01:00:41.837
and then with the
lovely baby raccoons

01:00:41.837 --> 01:00:44.373
coming up on stage
every once in a while.

01:00:44.373 --> 01:00:47.976
During Twelfth Night, the baby
raccoons came up on stage.

01:00:47.976 --> 01:00:50.146
And Anne Hathaway and I
thought that we were really

01:00:50.146 --> 01:00:51.013
killing the audience.

01:00:51.013 --> 01:00:52.315
They were just laughing.

01:00:52.315 --> 01:00:54.383
We were like, oh,
we're nailing it!

01:00:54.383 --> 01:00:56.819
We come off stage, and we're
like, "We nailed that scene!"

01:00:56.819 --> 01:00:58.521
And the cast was like,
"No, the raccoons

01:00:58.521 --> 01:01:01.823
had come on behind you."

01:01:01.823 --> 01:01:04.159
But yes, any time that
Oskar will have us,

01:01:04.159 --> 01:01:05.995
Will and I will come for sure.

01:01:05.995 --> 01:01:07.430
That'll happen.

01:01:07.430 --> 01:01:08.464
Thank you.

01:01:08.464 --> 01:01:09.765
Well done.

01:01:09.765 --> 01:01:13.603
That's your membership
dues for the year.

01:01:13.603 --> 01:01:14.403
Hi.

01:01:14.403 --> 01:01:16.405
Hi.

01:01:16.405 --> 01:01:17.440
OK, I'm going
to take a deep breath.

01:01:18.941 --> 01:01:22.377
You've been a hero
of mine really

01:01:22.377 --> 01:01:23.880
ever since I heard your voice.

01:01:23.880 --> 01:01:27.016
I think there was a Leonard
Bernstein New York tribute

01:01:27.016 --> 01:01:28.217
album.

01:01:28.217 --> 01:01:29.451
And I heard you sing
"A Little Bit In Love,"

01:01:29.451 --> 01:01:30.953
and I was more than
a little bit in love.

01:01:30.953 --> 01:01:33.356
Oh, thank you.

01:01:33.356 --> 01:01:37.693
And you were a hero
of mine in the way

01:01:37.693 --> 01:01:41.764
you combined your classical
training with musical theater.

01:01:41.764 --> 01:01:44.767
And it was just sort
of a bolt hearing

01:01:44.767 --> 01:01:46.869
you talk about your
college experience

01:01:46.869 --> 01:01:48.403
and sort of
struggling to combine

01:01:48.403 --> 01:01:50.673
these and kind of
the different camps

01:01:50.673 --> 01:01:52.774
that music departments go into.

01:01:52.774 --> 01:01:55.277
And I struggled with that too.

01:01:55.277 --> 01:01:57.179
And I ended up leaving
a music department.

01:01:57.179 --> 01:02:02.118
But I guess, a, would you have
any advice for college students

01:02:05.821 --> 01:02:07.123
maybe struggling with that now?

01:02:07.123 --> 01:02:08.624
Or do you get the
sense that it's

01:02:08.624 --> 01:02:10.593
a little better now that
it's not kind of broken up

01:02:10.593 --> 01:02:12.327
in camps in this way?

01:02:12.327 --> 01:02:17.333
Well, I can't speak to
every college department.

01:02:18.901 --> 01:02:22.505
I do know that there have been
a lot of changes implemented

01:02:22.505 --> 01:02:24.774
at Julliard since the
years that I've been there.

01:02:24.774 --> 01:02:29.111
And they allow for the
younger vocal students

01:02:29.111 --> 01:02:31.680
to have some acting classes.

01:02:31.680 --> 01:02:34.317
Or they allow them to sing a
little bit of musical theater.

01:02:34.317 --> 01:02:36.051
They give them
performance opportunities

01:02:36.051 --> 01:02:39.922
now that were not available
to me in my generation there.

01:02:39.922 --> 01:02:44.927
And that's, I think, made for
a healthier sort of learning

01:02:45.961 --> 01:02:47.496
environment for these kids.

01:02:47.496 --> 01:02:51.634
And what my advice to this
students has always been--

01:02:51.634 --> 01:02:54.970
listen and learn as much as
you can, but trust your gut

01:02:54.970 --> 01:02:58.540
and do not try to sound
like anybody else.

01:02:58.540 --> 01:03:01.910
Try to sound like the best
version of you, but do not try

01:03:01.910 --> 01:03:03.145
and imitate.

01:03:03.145 --> 01:03:04.814
Do not try to sound
like anybody else.

01:03:04.814 --> 01:03:09.118
And that is what will set
you apart from everybody else

01:03:09.118 --> 01:03:11.420
is that you have your
own unique sound.

01:03:11.420 --> 01:03:13.656
And once I learned
that, that's what sort

01:03:13.656 --> 01:03:15.157
of kind of send me free.

01:03:15.157 --> 01:03:17.959
And so that's what I say to my
students more than anything.

01:03:17.959 --> 01:03:19.162
I don't know if it's helpful.

01:03:19.162 --> 01:03:20.162
Yeah, thank you.

01:03:20.162 --> 01:03:21.097
Thank you.

01:03:21.097 --> 01:03:23.565
[APPLAUSE]

01:03:23.565 --> 01:03:24.833
Hello.

01:03:24.833 --> 01:03:28.370
Hi, 24 years ago, I
saw you in Carousel.

01:03:28.370 --> 01:03:31.040
And it's an indelible memory.

01:03:31.040 --> 01:03:33.609
And it was a
wonderful performance.

01:03:33.609 --> 01:03:35.244
And I still remember
that so vividly.

01:03:35.244 --> 01:03:36.612
And I'm going to remember
that until the day I die.

01:03:36.612 --> 01:03:37.779
I really will.

01:03:37.779 --> 01:03:38.814
Thank you so much.

01:03:38.814 --> 01:03:39.948
Thank you.

01:03:39.948 --> 01:03:43.619
Lizzie in 110 Degrees
in the Shade--

01:03:43.619 --> 01:03:45.954
you're a very
high-octane person,

01:03:45.954 --> 01:03:48.991
and Lizzie is a plain character.

01:03:48.991 --> 01:03:52.227
What was your
process in deciding

01:03:52.227 --> 01:03:54.897
how to present
this plainness when

01:03:54.897 --> 01:03:58.201
it wasn't inherent in
the way you self-present?

01:03:58.201 --> 01:04:00.402
Well, I think the interesting
thing about Lizzie

01:04:00.402 --> 01:04:02.237
is she's not a plain character.

01:04:02.237 --> 01:04:07.210
That's how everybody in her
life kept describing her to her.

01:04:09.945 --> 01:04:13.616
And that's how she was sort of
branded within the community.

01:04:13.616 --> 01:04:15.283
You're plain.

01:04:15.283 --> 01:04:16.952
She talked a little too much.

01:04:16.952 --> 01:04:19.521
She was too smart.

01:04:19.521 --> 01:04:24.226
And so for me, it was--

01:04:24.226 --> 01:04:27.330
I mean, in terms
of what she looked like, OK,

01:04:29.231 --> 01:04:32.268
so I didn't wear any makeup in
the show or anything like that.

01:04:32.268 --> 01:04:35.637
But I think Lizzie actually
was a high-octane person

01:04:35.637 --> 01:04:39.207
with a lot of passion
and a lot of love to give

01:04:39.207 --> 01:04:42.578
and a lot of dreams
and aspirations

01:04:42.578 --> 01:04:46.815
that she kind of felt were
constantly being pushed down.

01:04:46.815 --> 01:04:49.852
And so the struggle for
Lizzie is to truly believe,

01:04:49.852 --> 01:04:52.521
and that's what Starbuck gives
her is that she helps her

01:04:52.521 --> 01:04:55.323
to see that she is beautiful.

01:04:55.323 --> 01:04:57.926
She is deserving of love.

01:04:57.926 --> 01:05:00.629
She does have all
that within her.

01:05:00.629 --> 01:05:05.134
So I think it's quite
actually the opposite is

01:05:05.134 --> 01:05:06.402
that she was not that.

01:05:06.402 --> 01:05:07.870
She was not that.

01:05:07.870 --> 01:05:10.438
And the journey is discovering
that, that she's not that.

01:05:10.438 --> 01:05:12.007
Well, I'm glad I have the album.

01:05:12.007 --> 01:05:14.877
OK.

01:05:14.877 --> 01:05:15.945
Hi.

01:05:15.945 --> 01:05:17.179
Hi.

01:05:17.179 --> 01:05:20.215
So you've worked both
on stage and on screen,

01:05:20.215 --> 01:05:23.786
but you've also had your stage
work recorded as with Lady

01:05:23.786 --> 01:05:25.387
Day and the HBO recording.

01:05:25.387 --> 01:05:26.922
And I was wondering
if you could just

01:05:26.922 --> 01:05:29.057
speak a little bit to
that experience of taking

01:05:29.057 --> 01:05:31.560
what's somewhat more
ephemeral in nature

01:05:31.560 --> 01:05:34.162
as theatrical performances
are often described

01:05:34.162 --> 01:05:38.400
and how it was like to do
that for a single recording

01:05:38.400 --> 01:05:41.270
and what you think of kind of
recording theater in general?

01:05:41.270 --> 01:05:43.939
Although that's a very touchy
topic for a lot of people.

01:05:43.939 --> 01:05:45.241
I think it's very important.

01:05:45.241 --> 01:05:47.309
For me-- maybe it's
just me being selfish--

01:05:47.309 --> 01:05:50.179
there are performances that
we'll never have,

01:05:53.482 --> 01:05:55.350
because, I mean,
I would have loved

01:05:55.350 --> 01:05:59.254
to have seen Merman in Gypsy.

01:05:59.254 --> 01:06:02.091
Can you imagine
opening night of Hamlet

01:06:02.091 --> 01:06:06.328
with Burbage at
the Globe Theater.

01:06:06.328 --> 01:06:09.831
I mean, we'd kill
for a tape of that.

01:06:09.831 --> 01:06:12.134
I mean, I love that
Lincoln Center Library

01:06:12.134 --> 01:06:13.602
archives all the shows now.

01:06:13.602 --> 01:06:17.739
That's very important
that we have that history.

01:06:17.739 --> 01:06:22.444
Speaking to the first part of
your question, we did Lady Day.

01:06:22.444 --> 01:06:26.915
When we filmed it, we filmed
it at a kind of defunct cafe

01:06:26.915 --> 01:06:29.851
in New Orleans that
we were able to sort

01:06:29.851 --> 01:06:33.422
of turn into the nightclub.

01:06:33.422 --> 01:06:36.959
And it was very difficult.
It was very difficult.

01:06:36.959 --> 01:06:38.693
We did it over three
different nights.

01:06:38.693 --> 01:06:41.863
And we did have audience
members come in,

01:06:41.863 --> 01:06:43.965
because people would have
been in the audience.

01:06:43.965 --> 01:06:46.701
So that's the realistic
sort of portrayal.

01:06:46.701 --> 01:06:51.707
But it was very difficult,
because we also had 11 cameras.

01:06:52.574 --> 01:06:54.109
And that's a very tiny space.

01:06:54.109 --> 01:06:56.611
We had 11 cameras, and even like
when she would go off the stage

01:06:56.611 --> 01:06:58.147
and go to the bar
and stuff, I was

01:06:58.147 --> 01:07:01.450
having to kind of walk by and
over camera dollies and stuff

01:07:01.450 --> 01:07:03.185
like that.

01:07:03.185 --> 01:07:06.422
I had to
use different muscles.

01:07:08.524 --> 01:07:13.529
And if we're being completely
honest, I haven't seen it.

01:07:13.529 --> 01:07:14.763
Honestly, I haven't.

01:07:14.763 --> 01:07:16.264
And I think it's
going to be years

01:07:16.264 --> 01:07:18.767
before I'm able to
watch that performance,

01:07:18.767 --> 01:07:23.773
because I don't like
watching myself on film.

01:07:25.174 --> 01:07:29.378
And I know what I went through
in trying to stay focused

01:07:30.245 --> 01:07:31.579
to get that performance.

01:07:31.579 --> 01:07:33.415
So it would be uncomfortable
for me to watch.

01:07:33.415 --> 01:07:36.885
But it's a very
tricky thing to do.

01:07:36.885 --> 01:07:39.288
I don't think I
answered your question.

01:07:39.288 --> 01:07:40.322
You absolutely did.

01:07:40.322 --> 01:07:42.224
Thank you so much.

01:07:42.224 --> 01:07:44.092
I mean, one thing I
will say about that

01:07:44.092 --> 01:07:47.796
is, I am delighted that the
New York Library formats exist

01:07:47.796 --> 01:07:51.433
and delighted that we have the
theater on tape collection.

01:07:51.433 --> 01:07:54.303
And to me, it is an
academic research tool.

01:07:54.303 --> 01:07:55.236
Yes.

01:07:55.236 --> 01:07:56.972
I go up there to study stuff.

01:07:56.972 --> 01:08:00.108
I don't ever feel like
I'm going up there

01:08:00.108 --> 01:08:02.911
to have a complete
experience of a work of art.

01:08:02.911 --> 01:08:06.047
I'm going up there, and
it's a historical record

01:08:06.047 --> 01:08:10.485
of a work of art that
isn't there anymore.

01:08:10.485 --> 01:08:12.687
Because, I mean,
my parochial view

01:08:12.687 --> 01:08:16.591
is that the theater
is supposed to vanish.

01:08:16.591 --> 01:08:21.496
That's the whole truth
of it, just like we do.

01:08:21.496 --> 01:08:23.498
It vanishes.

01:08:23.498 --> 01:08:26.501
But that doesn't mean that I
don't love having it on tape.

01:08:26.501 --> 01:08:28.270
Absolutely.

01:08:28.270 --> 01:08:28.837
Hi.

01:08:28.837 --> 01:08:29.505
Hello.

01:08:29.505 --> 01:08:31.039
Thank you.

01:08:31.039 --> 01:08:34.310
You talked about thinking
and researching the roles,

01:08:34.310 --> 01:08:39.315
but I'm curious about practice
and how you practice or maybe

01:08:40.048 --> 01:08:42.417
rehearse in your field.

01:08:42.417 --> 01:08:47.423
And what's different about
that than when you're standing

01:08:49.023 --> 01:08:51.927
in the moment and performing?

01:08:51.927 --> 01:08:55.030
Do you mean in terms of
like rehearsal process?

01:08:55.030 --> 01:08:56.532
Yes.

01:08:56.532 --> 01:08:58.100
Well, I mean, when you're
working on any show, I mean,

01:08:58.100 --> 01:09:03.038
you rehearse usually
six to eight weeks.

01:09:04.439 --> 01:09:07.742
And with a Broadway schedule,
you rehearse 10:00 to 6:00

01:09:07.742 --> 01:09:11.613
every day, six days a week,
for six to eight weeks.

01:09:11.613 --> 01:09:14.616
And then once you're
up and in previews,

01:09:14.616 --> 01:09:17.553
before you officially open a
show, you do the show at night,

01:09:17.553 --> 01:09:20.622
and you rehearse
during the day as well.

01:09:20.622 --> 01:09:24.692
And aside from that, a
performer is like an athlete.

01:09:24.692 --> 01:09:27.362
You still have to take
your voice lessons.

01:09:27.362 --> 01:09:30.266
You have to keep yourself
in tip-top condition,

01:09:30.266 --> 01:09:34.236
because our body
is our instrument.

01:09:34.236 --> 01:09:39.241
And so Will and I both, we
still take voice lessons.

01:09:40.175 --> 01:09:41.443
When I was doing
Shuffle Along, I

01:09:41.443 --> 01:09:43.712
was having to learn how
to tap dance or remember

01:09:43.712 --> 01:09:44.947
how to tap dance.

01:09:44.947 --> 01:09:46.882
After 40 years of
not doing it, I

01:09:46.882 --> 01:09:49.751
had to figure out
how to do that again.

01:09:49.751 --> 01:09:51.219
So the training never stops.

01:09:51.219 --> 01:09:54.789
You're constantly in training.

01:09:54.789 --> 01:09:56.357
You had a second
part of the question.

01:09:56.357 --> 01:09:58.260
What's it like then
once you stand on stage?

01:09:58.260 --> 01:09:59.495
Here's the thing.

01:09:59.495 --> 01:10:00.696
Once you get up
on stage, you then

01:10:00.696 --> 01:10:03.398
have to throw it
all out the window.

01:10:03.398 --> 01:10:04.365
That's the truth.

01:10:04.365 --> 01:10:05.768
You throw it all out the window.

01:10:05.768 --> 01:10:07.336
And you're forced
to be in the moment.

01:10:07.336 --> 01:10:12.341
And I want to say you're
flying on blind faith and luck.

01:10:13.709 --> 01:10:16.578
But you do have to throw
it all out the window

01:10:16.578 --> 01:10:19.147
and then just go
down to your gut.

01:10:19.147 --> 01:10:21.549
Do you want to do it?

01:10:21.549 --> 01:10:22.685
No, but I think about it in an
athletic performative space.

01:10:28.891 --> 01:10:31.727
You don't rehearse the
marathon in the same way

01:10:31.727 --> 01:10:33.261
that you run the marathon.

01:10:33.261 --> 01:10:35.530
That's true, but you train
for a marathon, don't you?

01:10:35.530 --> 01:10:36.798
Yeah, absolutely.

01:10:36.798 --> 01:10:37.466
Absolutely.

01:10:37.466 --> 01:10:38.600
Thank you.

01:10:38.600 --> 01:10:40.269
Hi.

01:10:40.269 --> 01:10:44.906
One of my favorite plays
was Master Class with you.

01:10:44.906 --> 01:10:47.710
I'm missing your signature.

01:10:49.778 --> 01:10:54.650
I've been waiting 21, 22 years.

01:10:57.685 --> 01:10:59.120
23 years.

01:10:59.120 --> 01:11:02.591
But I do you have Zoe
Caldwell's signature

01:11:02.591 --> 01:11:05.193
and just wondered if
you would consider

01:11:05.193 --> 01:11:09.797
playing the part of Maria
Callas this time, or have you?

01:11:09.797 --> 01:11:11.600
First of all, yes, I
will sign your poster.

01:11:11.600 --> 01:11:14.469
I'm sorry I wasn't there
to sign it 22 years ago.

01:11:14.469 --> 01:11:16.438
Shame on me.

01:11:16.438 --> 01:11:20.408
And actually, that question
has been asked before,

01:11:20.408 --> 01:11:23.612
if I would want to
play Maria Callas.

01:11:23.612 --> 01:11:27.449
I think you would,
should, could.

01:11:27.449 --> 01:11:29.317
It's an amazing role.

01:11:29.317 --> 01:11:31.753
It's hard, because
there's a part of me that

01:11:31.753 --> 01:11:35.891
would absolutely love to do it,
but there's a part of me that--

01:11:35.891 --> 01:11:39.228
the performance by Zoe Caldwell,
who I named my first child

01:11:39.228 --> 01:11:44.233
after, is so sacred
to me that I don't

01:11:45.434 --> 01:11:46.401
know that I would
want to touch it,

01:11:46.401 --> 01:11:48.937
because it's so sacred to me--

01:11:48.937 --> 01:11:50.739
my relationship to
her, what she meant

01:11:50.739 --> 01:11:52.741
to me, what that whole
experience meant to me,

01:11:52.741 --> 01:11:54.743
and watching every
night her do it.

01:11:54.743 --> 01:11:58.747
There's a part
of me that feels that it might

01:12:00.649 --> 01:12:02.417
be sacrilegious to play her.

01:12:02.417 --> 01:12:05.620
It's my own personal
things, so I don't know.

01:12:05.620 --> 01:12:06.821
But it's an amazing role.

01:12:06.821 --> 01:12:08.456
I'm begging.

01:12:08.456 --> 01:12:12.027
I will sign your poster after.

01:12:13.629 --> 01:12:14.296
I promise.

01:12:14.296 --> 01:12:15.631
Thank you.

01:12:15.631 --> 01:12:18.934
Thank you.

01:12:18.934 --> 01:12:19.468
Hey!

01:12:19.468 --> 01:12:20.135
Hi.

01:12:20.135 --> 01:12:20.802
How are you?

01:12:20.802 --> 01:12:21.469
I'm good.

01:12:21.469 --> 01:12:22.003
How are you?

01:12:22.003 --> 01:12:23.271
Good.

01:12:23.271 --> 01:12:24.205
This is one of the
students that I've

01:12:24.205 --> 01:12:25.206
been working with this year.

01:12:25.206 --> 01:12:27.175
She has an amazing voice.

01:12:27.175 --> 01:12:28.677
Thank you.

01:12:28.677 --> 01:12:31.613
So last night you sang a
song from the perspective

01:12:31.613 --> 01:12:34.349
of a daughter whose mother is
a performer who is always away.

01:12:34.349 --> 01:12:38.253
And you mentioned kind of
your goal to not be that way.

01:12:38.253 --> 01:12:41.256
So I'm just curious, kind of a
personal question, how do you

01:12:41.256 --> 01:12:45.594
and your husband make that
not happen and make sure

01:12:45.594 --> 01:12:48.363
that that's not how
your children feel?

01:12:48.363 --> 01:12:53.369
Mike is right there.

01:12:56.972 --> 01:12:58.106
He actually is.

01:12:58.106 --> 01:13:00.375
[LAUGHTER]

01:13:00.375 --> 01:13:02.177
I'm sure you're both
wonderful parents.

01:13:02.177 --> 01:13:04.579
What's it like from
your perspective?

01:13:04.579 --> 01:13:07.349
Oh, it's like a
Chinese jigsaw puzzle.

01:13:07.349 --> 01:13:08.550
I don't know.

01:13:08.550 --> 01:13:11.119
We just sit down and
have planning meetings.

01:13:11.119 --> 01:13:12.587
You're going to
this soccer lesson.

01:13:12.587 --> 01:13:15.456
And I'm going to rehearsal.

01:13:15.456 --> 01:13:17.125
And you have to work this day.

01:13:17.125 --> 01:13:19.827
It's a bit like air
traffic control.

01:13:19.827 --> 01:13:21.363
That's a better metaphor.

01:13:21.363 --> 01:13:25.901
But I mean, Will and I, it's not
like we go out and party a lot.

01:13:27.536 --> 01:13:30.272
If we're not working,
we're really with the kids.

01:13:30.272 --> 01:13:31.807
But we also make
certain choices,

01:13:31.807 --> 01:13:35.944
like we don't really audition
for TV shows that shoot in LA

01:13:35.944 --> 01:13:38.880
so that we can be home.

01:13:38.880 --> 01:13:41.449
For a lot of actors, that would
minimize a whole lot of work.

01:13:41.449 --> 01:13:43.685
And years ago, it certainly
did, because there

01:13:43.685 --> 01:13:45.687
wasn't a lot of film and
television in New York.

01:13:45.687 --> 01:13:48.123
A lot of it was all LA,
and that's slowly changing.

01:13:48.123 --> 01:13:49.958
But yeah, absolutely.

01:13:49.958 --> 01:13:54.963
And it is a lot of planning and
one foot in front of the other

01:13:55.697 --> 01:13:57.232
as each day goes.

01:13:57.232 --> 01:14:01.537
But literally, when we're not
working, we're with the kids.

01:14:04.239 --> 01:14:09.244
And I mean, there's no
children present right now.

01:14:09.978 --> 01:14:10.912
Actually one is here.

01:14:10.912 --> 01:14:13.882
One did a tour of MIT today.

01:14:13.882 --> 01:14:16.284
Yeah, so maybe I'll be
giving the rest of the award

01:14:16.284 --> 01:14:17.018
right back to MIT.

01:14:17.018 --> 01:14:19.988
[LAUGHTER]

01:14:19.988 --> 01:14:22.991
[APPLAUSE]

01:14:22.991 --> 01:14:25.427
1632 01:14:23,657 --&gt;
01:14:24,240 You know what?

01:14:26.161 --> 01:14:27.429
Here's the thing.

01:14:27.429 --> 01:14:28.997
You commit to finding
a way to make it work.

01:14:28.997 --> 01:14:32.167
And you commit to being as good
a parent as you possibly can.

01:14:32.167 --> 01:14:33.602
And then you prioritize.

01:14:33.602 --> 01:14:36.170
And so it's like it's
work or the kids,

01:14:36.170 --> 01:14:38.606
and if they have other
plans, then we'll sneak away.

01:14:38.606 --> 01:14:41.810
But other than that,
it's work or the kids.

01:14:41.810 --> 01:14:43.044
Yeah, that's it.

01:14:43.044 --> 01:14:43.912
Thank you.

01:14:43.912 --> 01:14:44.613
You can do it!

01:14:44.613 --> 01:14:45.447
You can do it!

01:14:45.447 --> 01:14:48.583
But take your time.

01:14:48.583 --> 01:14:52.020
Could we get a microphone over--

01:14:52.020 --> 01:14:53.455
oh, there we go.

01:14:53.455 --> 01:14:54.823
Thank you.

01:14:54.823 --> 01:14:57.726
Hello, how are you?

01:14:58.961 --> 01:14:59.861
Nice to see you.

01:14:59.861 --> 01:15:01.062
It's good to see you.

01:15:01.062 --> 01:15:03.298
I'm wondering-- you
received this phone call

01:15:03.298 --> 01:15:06.969
from the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology

01:15:06.969 --> 01:15:10.571
to be told that you're going
to get a McDermott Award.

01:15:10.571 --> 01:15:15.577
And did you think they got the
wrong number, first of all?

01:15:16.944 --> 01:15:20.315
I'm just wondering if you could
tell us what surprised you

01:15:20.315 --> 01:15:21.817
about the residency?

01:15:21.817 --> 01:15:25.453
What surprised you about the
students and the faculty?

01:15:25.453 --> 01:15:27.789
What was your experience?

01:15:27.789 --> 01:15:32.794
You hear MIT, and if you're
as intelligent as I am,

01:15:35.363 --> 01:15:40.369
which is not much
intelligence, but I

01:15:41.503 --> 01:15:44.372
had a preconceived
idea or notion of what

01:15:44.372 --> 01:15:45.807
the students would be like.

01:15:45.807 --> 01:15:47.909
And I was telling Talia
this-- the student that I had

01:15:47.909 --> 01:15:48.843
the interview with--

01:15:48.843 --> 01:15:50.878
I was telling or this earlier.

01:15:50.878 --> 01:15:52.915
They've all
got their heads in books.

01:15:55.717 --> 01:16:00.088
Or they're blowing up
things in a science lab.

01:16:00.088 --> 01:16:02.858
What could
I possibly teach them?

01:16:04.526 --> 01:16:06.227
And are they even
going to be interested?

01:16:06.227 --> 01:16:11.233
And what I discovered
is these kids are--

01:16:12.834 --> 01:16:15.270
not only they do not have
their heads in books,

01:16:15.270 --> 01:16:17.839
and that's a lot of it,
because they have to.

01:16:17.839 --> 01:16:22.844
But they are wide eyed and
curious and precocious.

01:16:23.778 --> 01:16:28.149
And they seem to be full of joy.

01:16:28.149 --> 01:16:31.352
And what I got a sense
for all that was like--

01:16:31.352 --> 01:16:33.088
does this make sense?

01:16:33.088 --> 01:16:35.590
This is what I feel when
I think about these kids I

01:16:35.590 --> 01:16:40.195
see them reaching and arms
open and wanting to discover.

01:16:40.195 --> 01:16:42.798
And so for me, coming in
to spend time with them

01:16:42.798 --> 01:16:47.803
and talk to them and the few
master classes that I gave

01:16:49.170 --> 01:16:52.941
or helping, they were just
sponges full of joy and wonder.

01:16:54.209 --> 01:16:58.180
And I always felt
very hopeful when

01:16:58.180 --> 01:17:02.717
I left that if these kids
want to, not only figure out

01:17:02.717 --> 01:17:07.689
how to get us to Saturn,
cure cancer, and sing

01:17:07.689 --> 01:17:12.059
an aria all in one day, if
they want to do it, they can.

01:17:12.059 --> 01:17:14.095
They can.

01:17:14.095 --> 01:17:15.397
Yeah, it was very inspiring.

01:17:15.397 --> 01:17:17.699
[APPLAUSE]

01:17:17.699 --> 01:17:19.067
And what
I saw from the faculty
is that they give them

01:17:23.138 --> 01:17:25.273
the support and the space.

01:17:25.273 --> 01:17:28.343
And what I
didn't expect but what
I was thrilled to see is

01:17:33.949 --> 01:17:36.917
that there's a warmth that
comes from the faculty--

01:17:36.917 --> 01:17:40.321
the faculty that I've spent--

01:17:40.321 --> 01:17:43.091
there's a warmth
and a genuine caring

01:17:43.091 --> 01:17:44.592
that they have for
their students.

01:17:44.592 --> 01:17:46.994
And that filled me with
a great deal of hope,

01:17:46.994 --> 01:17:49.463
especially like watching the
way Laura works with her kids.

01:17:49.463 --> 01:17:53.801
You can tell she loves each and
every one of those kids so much

01:17:53.801 --> 01:17:57.739
and is there for
them and support.

01:17:57.739 --> 01:18:00.809
So especially as a
mom, two of our oldest

01:18:00.809 --> 01:18:02.443
are going off to
college in a year,

01:18:02.443 --> 01:18:05.246
to know that you could send
your kids to a place like this

01:18:05.246 --> 01:18:06.815
or wherever you go,
and there's going

01:18:06.815 --> 01:18:11.252
to be the support and warmth and
love as well as the knowledge

01:18:11.252 --> 01:18:13.221
that they need.

01:18:13.221 --> 01:18:14.656
I was inspired.

01:18:14.656 --> 01:18:18.393
I'm a convert.

01:18:18.994 --> 01:18:21.395
[LAUGHTER]

01:18:21.395 --> 01:18:24.666
If I can just add, I
came here last night.

01:18:24.666 --> 01:18:27.568
I was in Vancouver speaking
at the Ted Conference.

01:18:27.568 --> 01:18:29.070
And I'm surprised
anybody is here,

01:18:29.070 --> 01:18:31.506
because it felt like the entire
MIT faculty was presenting

01:18:31.506 --> 01:18:33.675
at the Ted Conference.

01:18:33.675 --> 01:18:37.946
But the kind of
technological genius

01:18:37.946 --> 01:18:39.647
that I was hearing
over the past few days

01:18:39.647 --> 01:18:42.751
in Vancouver, the idea
that can be coupled

01:18:42.751 --> 01:18:46.253
with an understanding
of the importance of art

01:18:46.253 --> 01:18:49.324
and the importance of
what culture means,

01:18:49.324 --> 01:18:52.494
not just in general, but
particular to research,

01:18:52.494 --> 01:18:57.499
particularly the idea of
science and advancement.

01:18:58.667 --> 01:18:59.600
Some of you are old
enough to remember

01:18:59.600 --> 01:19:01.503
CP Snow's The Two Cultures.

01:19:01.503 --> 01:19:05.640
The more that we can get
the culture and the science,

01:19:05.640 --> 01:19:08.510
the technology, and the
art to speak to each other,

01:19:08.510 --> 01:19:12.514
the more likely we are to have
a world that we want to live in.

01:19:12.514 --> 01:19:14.949
[APPLAUSE]

01:19:14.949 --> 01:19:18.820
1734 01:19:17,074 --&gt;
01:19:19,510 Hello.

01:19:21.256 --> 01:19:22.757
Hi.

01:19:22.757 --> 01:19:27.763
I loved, in the video reel,
hearing you talk about

01:19:29.230 --> 01:19:31.232
if there's a role or something
that you're scared of that you

01:19:31.232 --> 01:19:35.269
have to do it, because
it'll make you better,

01:19:35.269 --> 01:19:37.038
even if it's not successful.

01:19:37.038 --> 01:19:40.608
So I was wondering if
there was a time that

01:19:40.608 --> 01:19:43.979
came to mind that you can
think of when you were working

01:19:43.979 --> 01:19:46.848
through that, where you were
doing something you were scared

01:19:46.848 --> 01:19:48.583
of and then maybe
wasn't as successful

01:19:48.583 --> 01:19:50.852
as you would have hoped.

01:19:50.852 --> 01:19:53.187
And what would you say
to somebody who might

01:19:53.187 --> 01:19:56.224
be in that space right now?

01:19:56.224 --> 01:19:57.526
I would say to you.

01:19:57.526 --> 01:19:59.995
Wait, come here.

01:19:59.995 --> 01:20:01.630
Don't go away.

01:20:01.630 --> 01:20:02.464
Where are you going?

01:20:02.464 --> 01:20:03.398
Come here.

01:20:03.398 --> 01:20:04.866
[LAUGHTER]

01:20:04.866 --> 01:20:06.835
No, I mean, you don't
have stand back up there.

01:20:06.835 --> 01:20:08.436
I just wanted to get
out the spotlight.

01:20:08.436 --> 01:20:10.338
That's all.

01:20:10.338 --> 01:20:14.042
Ask and leave.

01:20:14.042 --> 01:20:17.679
I would say don't be
afraid of failure.

01:20:17.679 --> 01:20:21.982
The failure is part of
the learning experience.

01:20:21.982 --> 01:20:25.253
And I failed on a daily basis.

01:20:25.253 --> 01:20:29.224
On a daily basis, you fail.

01:20:29.224 --> 01:20:30.725
If you succeed at
everything, you

01:20:30.725 --> 01:20:33.695
won't ever grow, because
you won't ever learn.

01:20:33.695 --> 01:20:35.263
I firmly believe that.

01:20:35.263 --> 01:20:39.501
I'll be personal.

01:20:40.401 --> 01:20:41.903
Let me give you an example.

01:20:41.903 --> 01:20:46.508
As recently as yesterday,
I was scared to death

01:20:48.543 --> 01:20:50.779
to do Laura's piece
at the concert

01:20:50.779 --> 01:20:54.416
last night, because we had
only rehearsed it once.

01:20:54.416 --> 01:20:57.318
And I felt like, I don't want
to let Laura down and not

01:20:57.318 --> 01:20:59.387
do it here.

01:20:59.387 --> 01:21:00.421
This is her home.

01:21:00.421 --> 01:21:01.622
This is where she's teaching.

01:21:01.622 --> 01:21:02.857
This is where the kids are.

01:21:02.857 --> 01:21:04.793
And this is where I
learned the piece.

01:21:04.793 --> 01:21:09.163
But I'm not ready,
and I'm scared.

01:21:09.163 --> 01:21:11.366
And so at the point
in the concert

01:21:11.366 --> 01:21:13.501
when it was time to do it,
I turned to the pianist,

01:21:13.501 --> 01:21:15.536
and he said, "Do
you want to do it?"

01:21:15.536 --> 01:21:18.340
And I said, "I'm gonna mess up."

01:21:18.340 --> 01:21:19.907
And he was like,
OK, and he started

01:21:19.907 --> 01:21:21.709
to turn the page
to the next song.

01:21:21.709 --> 01:21:25.146
And then I thought, you're
scared, you need to do it.

01:21:25.146 --> 01:21:27.549
And I turned back to him
and I said, "Let's do it."

01:21:27.549 --> 01:21:30.117
So last night.

01:21:30.117 --> 01:21:31.352
And I get it all right.

01:21:31.352 --> 01:21:32.587
I mean, some of the
kids that know it,

01:21:32.587 --> 01:21:33.888
I messed up on a
lot of the lyrics,

01:21:33.888 --> 01:21:35.556
but oh, well, sorry, Laura.

01:21:35.556 --> 01:21:37.191
I'll get it right by May 1st.

01:21:37.191 --> 01:21:38.292
I will.

01:21:38.292 --> 01:21:39.193
But it doesn't matter.

01:21:39.193 --> 01:21:40.862
But I'm glad I did it.

01:21:40.862 --> 01:21:44.231
And I learned, and I fell
a little bit on my face.

01:21:44.231 --> 01:21:45.733
And you know what?

01:21:45.733 --> 01:21:47.702
And so instead of just hiding
all that from the audience,

01:21:47.702 --> 01:21:50.538
I told the audience like,
"I've rehearsed this once.

01:21:50.538 --> 01:21:51.572
I need to do it.

01:21:51.572 --> 01:21:52.841
Let's see what happens."

01:21:52.841 --> 01:21:53.875
And I said to the
kids, "If I mess up,

01:21:53.875 --> 01:21:54.976
shout out the words to me.

01:21:54.976 --> 01:21:56.344
We'll get through it."

01:21:56.344 --> 01:21:57.678
So fail every day.

01:21:57.678 --> 01:21:58.646
You'll learn something.

01:21:58.646 --> 01:22:01.049
[APPLAUSE]

01:22:01.049 --> 01:22:01.950
Now you can go.

01:22:03.351 --> 01:22:06.254
 Hey.

01:22:06.821 --> 01:22:07.388
Hi.

01:22:07.388 --> 01:22:08.623
Hi.

01:22:08.623 --> 01:22:10.025
First of all, I want
to say thank you,

01:22:10.025 --> 01:22:11.492
because when I was
in seventh grade

01:22:11.492 --> 01:22:13.795
I saw you in Porgy
and Bess in New York,

01:22:13.795 --> 01:22:16.998
and that's probably 90% of the
reason why I started singing.

01:22:16.998 --> 01:22:18.566
Oh, my goodness.

01:22:18.566 --> 01:22:21.002
And then my question
is, as a singer

01:22:21.002 --> 01:22:22.737
who mainly has
classical training,

01:22:22.737 --> 01:22:24.839
I was wondering if you
have any tips for expanding

01:22:24.839 --> 01:22:29.144
to other vocal genres without
losing touch with my own voice.

01:22:29.144 --> 01:22:34.149
Sing what you love.

01:22:38.453 --> 01:22:40.288
Does that make sense?

01:22:40.288 --> 01:22:43.591
Sing what you love.

01:22:43.591 --> 01:22:48.228
I mean, sometimes at home I'm
singing Earth, Wind, and Fire.

01:22:48.228 --> 01:22:50.665
I love Earth, Wind, and Fire.

01:22:50.665 --> 01:22:55.470
And so it's coming from a
place of joy in my heart.

01:22:55.470 --> 01:22:59.006
I mean, you fancy yourself
a singer absolutely, right?

01:22:59.006 --> 01:23:01.275
It's a part of who you are.

01:23:01.275 --> 01:23:03.011
So if you sing from
a place of love

01:23:03.011 --> 01:23:05.013
and sing from a place of
something that you want

01:23:05.013 --> 01:23:07.181
and that gives you
joy, just do it.

01:23:07.181 --> 01:23:09.417
The only thing I
would say is, as long

01:23:09.417 --> 01:23:14.021
as you're singing with your
voice, that's all that matters.

01:23:14.021 --> 01:23:16.391
Don't try and be anybody else.

01:23:16.391 --> 01:23:20.094
I don't sing Earth, Wind,
and Fire like Philip Bailey.

01:23:20.094 --> 01:23:22.263
I wish I could sing like
Philip Bailey, but I don't.

01:23:22.263 --> 01:23:23.731
I mean, we sing in
the same octave,

01:23:23.731 --> 01:23:24.933
because he sang very high.

01:23:24.933 --> 01:23:26.968
But I sing like me.

01:23:26.968 --> 01:23:29.170
So I'm bringing my own
interpretation to it.

01:23:29.170 --> 01:23:30.705
But I love it, and
I want to sing it,

01:23:30.705 --> 01:23:33.608
and I have something to
say about that music,

01:23:33.608 --> 01:23:36.910
about "September Song" or
"Got to Get You Into My Life,"

01:23:36.910 --> 01:23:38.446
whatever.

01:23:38.446 --> 01:23:40.415
It doesn't matter that I'm
singing it with my voice.

01:23:40.415 --> 01:23:45.420
So stick with your own voice,
but sing what you want.

01:23:46.020 --> 01:23:46.688
Thank you.

01:23:46.688 --> 01:23:49.457
[APPLAUSE]

01:23:49.457 --> 01:23:52.794
You were
at the lunch too.

01:23:53.962 --> 01:23:55.797
Yes, yes, you're back lit.

01:23:55.797 --> 01:23:56.631
Now I see your face.

01:23:56.631 --> 01:23:58.600
Hi.

01:23:58.600 --> 01:24:00.735
Did we had like
flan or something

01:24:00.735 --> 01:24:01.669
when we had the lunch?

01:24:01.669 --> 01:24:02.603
Creme brulee.

01:24:02.603 --> 01:24:03.405
The creme brulee, that's right.

01:24:03.405 --> 01:24:05.306
Yes, yes, yes.

01:24:05.306 --> 01:24:06.440
Hello.

01:24:06.440 --> 01:24:07.141
Hello.

01:24:07.141 --> 01:24:08.576
Hi.

01:24:08.576 --> 01:24:10.812
I was wondering-- so
like [INAUDIBLE] research

01:24:10.812 --> 01:24:12.380
like your roles and stuff.

01:24:12.380 --> 01:24:14.949
And a lot of them like have
a lot of historical context

01:24:14.949 --> 01:24:16.417
and all that.

01:24:16.417 --> 01:24:20.854
I remember my high school
did Ragtime my freshman year.

01:24:20.854 --> 01:24:23.090
So the soundtrack to
that was where I first

01:24:23.090 --> 01:24:23.925
learned about you.

01:24:23.925 --> 01:24:25.326
And it was amazing.

01:24:25.326 --> 01:24:29.697
So I was wondering when
you do all your research

01:24:29.697 --> 01:24:32.100
and you have all the
context and the background,

01:24:32.100 --> 01:24:35.203
how do you balance
that information

01:24:35.203 --> 01:24:37.705
with your own
interpretations when

01:24:37.705 --> 01:24:39.473
you're performing the roles?

01:24:39.473 --> 01:24:41.242
How do I balance
all the information

01:24:41.242 --> 01:24:42.743
from all the research
that I've done

01:24:42.743 --> 01:24:44.245
with my own interpretation?

01:24:44.245 --> 01:24:48.249
Yeah, like how you insert
yourself into the role.

01:24:48.249 --> 01:24:51.752
The research is
there to support you.

01:24:51.752 --> 01:24:53.287
You know what I mean?

01:24:53.287 --> 01:24:55.690
But in the end, the role has
to be filtered through you.

01:24:55.690 --> 01:25:00.594
So you take that
research, and it's there,

01:25:00.594 --> 01:25:03.130
and it's there to
support you as you then

01:25:03.130 --> 01:25:05.366
plug it into your own soul.

01:25:05.366 --> 01:25:07.535
And then in the end, you
can't take all those books

01:25:07.535 --> 01:25:09.871
and recordings and everything
out on the stage with you.

01:25:09.871 --> 01:25:11.272
You go, "All right.

01:25:11.272 --> 01:25:12.974
I am a play Billie Holiday,
but let me show you

01:25:12.974 --> 01:25:15.777
how I swear this is right.

01:25:15.777 --> 01:25:17.412
Check this out."

01:25:17.412 --> 01:25:19.680
In the end, that's why I'm
saying, when I said to him,

01:25:19.680 --> 01:25:20.915
you've got to throw
it all out the window.

01:25:20.915 --> 01:25:23.051
It's there to support
you in that phase of it,

01:25:23.051 --> 01:25:24.586
but then at the end, you
throw it out the window.

01:25:24.586 --> 01:25:26.821
And then it's how it then
filters through yourself.

01:25:26.821 --> 01:25:30.024
So in the end, it
does become up to you,

01:25:30.024 --> 01:25:33.294
and it becomes a
part of who you are.

01:25:33.294 --> 01:25:34.662
I would imagine--

01:25:34.662 --> 01:25:36.631
I don't know about the
sciences-- but I would imagine,

01:25:36.631 --> 01:25:38.866
in some way, you can
look at all the research,

01:25:38.866 --> 01:25:40.868
but then you have to come
to your own conclusion

01:25:40.868 --> 01:25:42.737
about what all the
research is showing you.

01:25:42.737 --> 01:25:44.405
It has to be your
conclusion in the end.

01:25:44.405 --> 01:25:45.706
So I think maybe it's the same.

01:25:45.706 --> 01:25:46.374
Thank you.

01:25:46.374 --> 01:25:47.942
I don't know.

01:25:47.942 --> 01:25:50.911
[APPLAUSE]

01:25:50.911 --> 01:25:55.917
So Audra, first, congratulationn
the 2018 Eugene McDermott

01:26:02.389 --> 01:26:04.225
Award in the arts at MIT.

01:26:04.225 --> 01:26:05.726
You're spectacular.

01:26:05.726 --> 01:26:07.228
You've honored us.

01:26:07.228 --> 01:26:10.297
And you brought Oskar with
you to double honor us.

01:26:10.297 --> 01:26:12.933
And the two of you are
phenomenal together.

01:26:12.933 --> 01:26:16.604
And you could see this
audience just deeply

01:26:16.604 --> 01:26:19.440
appreciates what you've done
for us on this occasion.

01:26:19.440 --> 01:26:20.374
Thank you so much.

01:26:20.374 --> 01:26:22.743
And please stay close to us.

01:26:22.743 --> 01:26:23.277
I will.

01:26:23.277 --> 01:26:23.944
I will.

01:26:23.944 --> 01:26:24.512
Both of you.

01:26:24.512 --> 01:26:25.179
We will.

01:26:25.179 --> 01:26:25.847
[APPLAUSE]

01:26:25.847 --> 01:26:27.815
Thank you.

01:26:27.815 --> 01:26:28.716
Well done.

01:26:28.716 --> 01:26:29.383
Well done.

01:26:29.383 --> 01:26:31.152
Thank you.

